WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.770
We'll continue on duality
which is this actually

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.650
super interesting topic.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.350
And what we've seen so
far, just to remind you,

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.000
is we take any optimization
problem at all, convex or not,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.160
and we form this thing
called the Lagrangian.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.610
And you can mumble about
what's the Lagrangian.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:28.180
You go, well, it's a way to
incorporate the constraints

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.700
into the objective.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.500
And you can mumble
about, oh, that's

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.550
the market based thing,
blah, blah, blah.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.800
OK, they could do this.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.960
And it's got these
mysterious weights,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.420
which is how much you weight the
constraint functions in forming

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.590
this Lagrangian, which is this
thing that includes some costs

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:51.460
or whatever additional
terms from the objectives.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.270
Sorry, from the constraints.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.460
So that's the Lagrangian.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.290
And then from that we
get a dual function.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:01.540
And to get a dual function you
minimize over x, your variable.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.260
And that gives you
this dual function,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.880
which is a function of
these Lagrange multipliers.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.360
And then we observed
that this gives you

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.410
a lower bound on the optimal
value of the original problem,

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:15.950
everybody.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.200
So just to recap where we are.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.710
And so you can even think of
that as a parametrized lower

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.480
bound on the optimal value.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.510
It's parametrized
by lambda and nu.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.233
And so in other words,
for any lambda nu,

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:29.150
you get a lower bound.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.620
Now by the way,
in a lot of cases,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.820
if you just throw in
random lambdas and news,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.870
here's the lower bound
you get minus infinity.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.640
But that's a
universal lower bound

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.410
and is utterly uninformative.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.920
But in other cases, what you
get is not obvious at all.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.990
So here's an example.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.990
So this is the
partitioning problem.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.210
I think we talked
about it last time.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:55.380
And it's not convex for
a couple of reasons.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.280
Number one, we didn't say that
W was positive semi-definite.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.260
So your objective does
not have to be convex.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.690
And then the other
one which makes

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.450
this very much not
a convex problem is

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.139
that we have a square equals 1.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.180
And you can't do that
in a convex problem.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:16.370
Equality constraints
must be affine.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.510
So that's the problem.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.590
And here what we're
going to do is work out

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.500
the dual function for it.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.400
And to do that we simply form,
this is the Lagrangian here,

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.560
and we minimize this over x.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.500
And when we minimize
it over x we

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.200
find out that it's a
constant plus this thing

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.670
which is a quadratic form.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.760
And the minimum of a
quadratic form over x

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.230
is either 0 if the quadratic
form is positive semidefinite.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.630
I mean, that's
clearly the minimum.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.930
Or that quadratic
form, if that matrix

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.920
has one negative eigenvalue,
the minimum is minus infinity.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.420
Then at that point,
it's game over.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:57.950
It's minus infinity.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:03.230
So you end up with a very
interesting lower bound.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.550
It says that if you come
up with some vectors--

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.440
sorry, a vector
nu or some numbers

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.950
nu i, and provided
this holds, if you

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.540
can take your original matrix,
add this diagonal matrix,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.550
and if that's
positive semidefinite,

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:23.877
then minus 1 transpose nu is
a lower bound on this problem.

00:03:23.877 --> 00:03:25.960
That's actually super
interesting because actually

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.680
solving this problem
exactly is extremely hard.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.540
Good news is you
almost never have

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.800
to solve it exactly so I'm
not too concerned about that.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.590
I mean, for one reason, if
you're doing partitioning,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.720
somebody made up these W's.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.670
And then if you say I
haven't solved it exactly,

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.340
someone would say, oh, you
have to solve it exactly.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.810
Then you say to them, where
did your W's come from

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.560
and they tell you.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.910
And the truth is usually,
they just made them up.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.550
I mean, the sign
is generally right.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.830
But generally,
they're just made up.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.350
So it's not that big
a deal in practice

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.110
that you can't solve
this problem exactly.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.220
And let me just give
a quick-- there's

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.840
a lot of heuristics for this.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.520
I think I even
mentioned one last time,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.350
it's based on
spectral partitioning,

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:13.350
but there's a lot.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.933
And the other thing
is you could write

00:04:14.933 --> 00:04:16.760
like a super dumb greedy method.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.200
Just like the
dumbest thing ever.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:18.829
Here's my method.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:19.250
Ready?

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.529
I'm just saying this is if
you were doing Street Fighting

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.660
stuff, and you're at an
internship, or a startup,

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:24.660
and you need to do this.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.150
Well, what you'd
really do is you'd

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.210
go find some stuff on GitHub or
whatever and start from there.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.720
But the point is what you
might do for something

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.940
like this is you initialize
x with a random plus/minus 1

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:36.440
vector.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.490
Everybody got it?

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.020
Now, here's my algorithm.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.670
It's incredibly dumb.

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.360
I cycle through the
indexes 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.380
And I simply ask myself
if I flip the sign of x7,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.780
will the objective go down.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.800
If it goes down, I take
it, and I keep going.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.410
And I keep doing this
cycling through until this

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.010
is stationary.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:04:59.390
Everybody got that?

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:03.120
Then if I want to write
another four lines of code,

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.210
I can start doing
things like this.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:09.410
I'll look at pairs and think
about simultaneous swaps

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.643
and cycle until there
are no pairs that

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:12.560
improve the objective.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.120
And then I guess in the--

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.530
I guess in the--

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.730
I guess it's called in--

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.060
I guess, computer science refers
to the first one as one opt

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.100
and the second is two opt.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.950
One opt means that
there is no change

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.340
of a single variable that
will improve your objective.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.080
And the second one is two opt.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.210
There is no change
of any two variables

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.920
that would improve
your objective.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.270
It doesn't mean you're optimal.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.080
It just means you're locally
optimal in this discrete set,

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.910
which is a--

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.350
anyway, everybody got that?

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:46.670
So methods like that actually
often work really well.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.710
Then what's
interesting is then--

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.420
I mean, sometimes
you don't care.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.498
You put it into
some big old project

00:05:53.498 --> 00:05:54.540
and everything's working.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:55.500
And it's really cool.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.978
And no one really cares.

00:05:56.978 --> 00:05:58.020
You test it a little bit.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.790
You make that kind of stuff.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:01.450
But no one really cares.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:05.250
But you might ask, how
far from optimal is it?

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:08.160
And then it would be something
like this that would give you--

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.200
that would give you a very--

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.448
I mean, give you, in some
cases, a pretty good bound.

00:06:12.448 --> 00:06:14.490
I mean, of course, it
depends on your [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.390
So what would
happen is you'd say,

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:20.490
well, I have a heuristic
runs in 50 milliseconds.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.233
And you'd say, oh, yeah, well,
I mean on this problem instance,

00:06:23.233 --> 00:06:24.150
what value did it get?

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.890
You go, oh, I got this
thing down to 3.2.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.770
And you go, OK,
well, that's cool.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.020
How far from optimal is that?

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.940
And you'd go, yeah,
well, I came up

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.580
with a lower bound,
provable, that

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.830
says no one could
do better than 2.7.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.150
Everybody follow this?

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.130
That's actually really
useful because it

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.050
means in that project--

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.360
I mean you probably
don't need to someone--

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.980
you don't need
someone to go in there

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.870
and improve your partitioner.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:52.843
They should focus--

00:06:52.843 --> 00:06:53.760
Everybody seeing this?

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.485
I'm just saying
these are already

00:06:55.485 --> 00:06:57.315
super interesting
and practical things.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.080
So there's a strong
connection to things

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.270
like conjugate functions.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:12.430
And to see that, this is
just a completely generic--

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.440
that's just an
optimization problem.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.710
Not convex, by the way.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.080
It doesn't have to be.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.210
This is not necessarily
a convex problem.

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.890
It's got linear
inequality constraints

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:25.000
so you have a
polyhedral feasible set.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.840
Of course that's convex.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:27.880
That's fine.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.400
And we have an objective
we want to minimize.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.710
So if you work out
the dual function,

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.570
well, it's f0 of x plus--

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.550
the Lagrangian is f0 of x plus--

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.910
this is going to be here, we
write a-- here I write this

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:41.260
as--

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.780
we're writing it all
in terms of lambda--

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.130
sorry, x.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.310
This is lambda
transpose Ax minus B,

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:51.130
and then plus nu
transpose Cx minus d.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.720
And that ends up with
you write it this way.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:56.560
And then here, we have
to minimize over x.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.780
And a lot of stuff floating
around here but here's

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:00.590
what you see.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.760
These terms don't
depend on x at all.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.920
So we don't care.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.340
They pop out of the infimum.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:10.340
Then this thing is,
minimize a function

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.160
plus a linear function.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:14.420
Well, that sounds familiar.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.740
As a matter of fact,
it's like the conjugate.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.950
So unfortunately,
the conjugate is

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.990
you're maximizing a linear
function minus your function.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.920
But these are the same things
with the appropriate changes

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:26.180
of sign.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:31.400
So what it says is
this thing is that.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:32.539
So there you go.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:36.086
So the conjugate
just came right out

00:08:36.086 --> 00:08:38.900
as a portion of
the dual function.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.179
Everybody got this?

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:44.870
So this is actually
quite useful.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.370
So here's a problem.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.500
If I take this thing,
this one's convex.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.860
Suppose that's my objective.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.380
This is the negative entropy.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.350
And for example, that would be
a super interesting problem.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:59.390
x might be a probability
distribution on n items.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:03.160
Presumably over here, we
have 1 transpose x equals 1.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:05.170
And somewhere over here
we-- well, actually we

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:06.920
don't need that because
the domain of this

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.240
says all the xs
are non-negative.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.250
But then the other constraints
here are basically the--

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:19.810
this would tell you there are
expected values of functions

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:21.430
whose value is given.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.814
And this would say
they're inequalities.

00:09:23.814 --> 00:09:28.570
So I could say, oh, the
second moment is this.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:34.450
The probability that x
satisfies this is 0.2.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:37.203
By the way, the third moment
lies between this number

00:09:37.203 --> 00:09:37.870
and that number.

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:38.832
Something like this.

00:09:38.832 --> 00:09:39.790
That would all be this.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:43.840
And then this problem would
be finding the maximum entropy

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:50.200
distribution consistent
with your requirements

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:51.370
on these expected values.

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:52.750
Everybody got that?

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:54.640
I mentioned this just
because that's actually

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.570
quite a real problem

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:02.330
So in this case, I mean you
could go through all of this

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.210
but there's no reason
to because you just go,

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.750
and I go to Google or
something, and type in conjugate

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.460
of negative entropy.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:13.280
And you will find out
that it's just this thing.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.410
And then that means that
the dual function of the--

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:19.370
You'd say it this way.

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:22.520
The dual function of a
maximum entropy problem

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:27.980
is a convex optimization problem
that involves exponentials.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.990
But by the way--

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:34.430
I mean, that should
actually strike a chord

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.170
to people in
statistics because it's

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:39.500
a connection between maximum
entropy and exponential

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:40.160
families.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.940
If you know what I'm
talking about, fine.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:43.790
And if you don't,
that's fine too.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:46.340
But just if you know
about that stuff.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.790
And in fact, there's a lot
of duality, and conjugates,

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:53.970
and things floating
around in those areas.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.850
So let me first
say where we are.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.220
So here's where we are.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:01.050
We have a method where you
have any problem at all

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.360
and we form this Lagrangian.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:04.590
Then we form dual function.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:06.382
And someone says, what's
the dual function?

00:11:06.382 --> 00:11:08.340
And you go, well,
it's a lower bound

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.290
on the optimal value
of your problem.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.700
You go, OK, cool.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.133
So then the most obvious
thing in the world

00:11:14.133 --> 00:11:16.050
is you'd say, well, what
are these lambda, nu?

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:17.970
And you go, there's parameters.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:19.400
And you go, well,
what do they do?

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:22.290
And you go, for different ones,
I get different lower bounds.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:25.800
But what you
obviously want to do

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:27.750
is find the best lower bound.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.940
I mean you should have
an urge to do that.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.757
Uncontrollable urge to do that.

00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:33.840
And that leads to this
problem, and this is called

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.440
the Lagrange dual problem.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.053
And so what it says
is, g of lambda nu

00:11:38.053 --> 00:11:39.720
is this lower bound
on the optimal value

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.270
of your original problem,
which might not be

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.025
convex, which is interesting.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:51.600
Then this says find
the largest lower bound

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.470
on the objective on the optimal
value of the original problem

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:59.515
using Lagrange duality.

00:11:59.515 --> 00:12:02.830
That's how you'd
say this in English.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:03.970
Here's what's cool.

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.920
This is actually
a convex problem

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.000
even if the original
problem was not So that's

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.270
actually kind of interesting.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:16.270
So here's an example is, here's
a so-called standard form

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:17.110
linear program.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:20.140
You minimize a linear function
subject to equality constraints

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:21.700
and non-negativity.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:24.070
And then you'll remember
that we worked out the dual.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.260
By the way, this is
not quite the du--

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:30.850
The dual here would be
maximize a function that

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:36.100
is minus infinity
unless you satisfy this

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:37.490
and plus infinity otherwise.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:42.700
So I've done a few innocent
translations of the problem

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.740
here.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:49.840
So what it says is, these two
LPs are intimately related,

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.170
that's what it says.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.310
It says, in fact,
they are duals.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.730
Actually, the name dual
suggests conjugate.

00:12:57.730 --> 00:12:59.980
It's something that
if you do it twice,

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.540
you get back to the original.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.720
And like conjugate,
that's kind of true.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.390
But I mean in some cases, right?

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.850
So we would say
these are dual LPs.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:11.580
Make sense?

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.525
And I mean these
things are not obvious.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.990
So now, we talk about
weak and strong dual--

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.730
So the first thing
you have is this.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.450
So d star is the optimal
value of the dual.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.510
And that means it's
the largest lower bound

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:33.410
on the optimal value
of your problem

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.620
that you can find using
Lagrangian duality.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:37.520
So that's what d star is.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:41.210
p star is the actual value--
is the actual optimal value

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:42.860
of the problem.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.890
And by the way, perfectly cool
to have either or both of those

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:47.300
be infinite.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:51.220
So for example, if
d star is infinity,

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.100
then p star has to be infinity.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:57.180
And basically,
that's a certificate

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:01.620
that tells you that the
original problem is infeasible.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:03.300
So this holds no matter what.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.080
That always holds.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.730
Convex problems,
non-convex, there

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.720
are no pathologies,
no exceptions.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:08.820
It just always holds.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:11.040
That's so-called weak duality.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.577
And it's based on these
very deep mathematical facts

00:14:14.577 --> 00:14:16.410
like the product of two
non-negative numbers

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.102
is non-negative.

00:14:17.102 --> 00:14:19.560
And if you add two non-negative
numbers, it's non-negative.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:22.260
So it looks fancy
and you can make

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.540
it sound fancy, and
weird, and mystical,

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.200
and say duality is this.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:29.910
But the point is, it's based
on just the simplest of things.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.740
But now it gets interesting.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:37.350
So strong duality is
when these two are equal.

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:40.980
So it says, there's
actually-- strong duality

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:46.380
says basically that
you can actually

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.670
find dual variables whose
associated lower bound

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.380
on the optimal value is actually
equal to the optimal value.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:56.430
This is strong duality.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.230
Now that in general
doesn't hold.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:14:59.310
But here's the cool part.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.957
It usually holds
for convex problems.

00:15:01.957 --> 00:15:03.540
And in fact, I'd
even strengthen that.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:08.250
I would say that in all
practical problems, convex

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:11.266
problems, it holds, all.

00:15:11.266 --> 00:15:13.990
Are there convex problems
for which it fails?

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:14.800
There are.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:17.710
But if you encounter one,
you better actually go back

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:18.460
and ask yourself--

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.860
I mean, number one,
you're not going to.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.260
But if you did-- actually,
what's much more likely

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:25.450
is some annoying
person who works

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:27.190
with you is going
to say, well, how

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:31.630
do you know-- how do you know
you have strong duality, right?

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:35.560
In which case you just
tell them to go away.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:40.540
But if you were to encounter
one of these pathologies,

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.220
you would need to
rethink your whole--

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:43.720
you're going to
have to rethink what

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:45.100
you're doing because you're
doing something wrong.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:46.900
That's basically the
right way to say it.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:56.300
Now to know that
strong duality holds,

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:59.098
you actually have to add
usually some constraint.

00:15:59.098 --> 00:16:00.890
These are called
constraint qualifications.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:04.370
There's entire books
on these things.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.010
You could take entire
courses on nothing

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.050
but constraint qualifications.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.360
And somewhere around
150th page of these books,

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.000
you see this big old thing
and they have people's names,

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.600
like Slater's method, and
this one, and that one.

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:19.700
And you would see
which implies which

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:22.856
and stuff like that,
which is all fine.

00:16:22.856 --> 00:16:27.350
I'm trained in pure maths
so I'm allowed to say things

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:28.340
about mathematics.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:29.150
It's totally fine.

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.130
I'm not hurting anyone.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:33.620
But it's not something I
think normal people need

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:36.840
to worry about, to be perfectly
honest But we'll get to that.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.590
So here's an example where--

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.935
this is actually
super interesting.

00:16:42.935 --> 00:16:45.530
First you write your three-line
thing, your greedy thing

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:46.760
that does partitioning.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.380
You test it.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:49.700
It passes all the unit tests.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.130
You plug it into the big
system, you're a hero.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:52.850
It works.

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:54.240
It's fast.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:55.710
And then at some
point you're bored

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.570
and someone says,
well, how far do

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.370
you think you are from
the global optimum?

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:00.930
And the answer is
you don't know.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:05.040
But what you do then is you
solve this convex problem here.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:08.339
That's actually a
semidefinite program.

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:11.160
And that will give you a number.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:14.160
And you compare it to the number
that your greedy algorithm

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.530
is producing for partitioning.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:20.349
And if they're reasonably
close and guess what?

00:17:20.349 --> 00:17:22.962
Of course they're going to
be reasonably close then

00:17:22.962 --> 00:17:24.670
you declare-- actually
it's kind of cool.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:26.753
Then you just set that
aside and wait for somebody

00:17:26.753 --> 00:17:29.580
to say, hey, how do you
do your partitioning.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:32.100
And you go, [SCOFFS]
four-line greedy algorithm,

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:34.140
or whatever you do you.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:35.753
I do spectral
partitioning followed

00:17:35.753 --> 00:17:37.170
with a four-line
greedy algorithm.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:38.045
And how does it work?

00:17:38.045 --> 00:17:40.080
And you go, really well, thanks.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:43.470
And then if they're
annoying, they would say,

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.330
but that's non-convex.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.940
How far are you from
the global optimum?

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.010
Probably the correct answer
to that is, who cares

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.403
and please go away because
I have actual work to do.

00:17:52.403 --> 00:17:53.820
But that's probably
the right one.

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:55.445
But you could say,
oh, funny you should

00:17:55.445 --> 00:17:57.960
ask that because I actually
solved the dual problem

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:01.500
and I found out I'm
typically about 5% to 10%

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:02.790
at most suboptimal.

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:05.880
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:06.570
Cool.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:11.280
So this is probably the
most famous constraint

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:12.540
qualification.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.700
I guess for people who
actually study these things,

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.590
this is the sledge hammer.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.560
So just, this is
a sledge hammer.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.200
It's just basically like,
this will do the trick.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:23.310
It's this.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:27.600
And it basically says
that strong duality

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:29.970
is guaranteed for
a convex problem

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:34.830
provided there exists a point
where the inequalities hold

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:35.580
strictly.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:37.990
Now there are lots of problems
where that doesn't hold

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:39.720
and yet strong
duality is just fine.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:48.370
And for example, you can
excuse the linear inequalities

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:49.750
from this requirement.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:55.180
That linear inequalities do not
need to be satisfied strictly.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:56.800
And there are a
gazillion other types

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:58.330
of constraint qualifications.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:00.040
So if you get into
a weird mood, you

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:02.830
can go to Google or
Wikipedia and type constraint

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:04.720
qualifications.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.390
I mean, if you're
interested in that.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:15.143
So let's look at something.

00:19:15.143 --> 00:19:16.810
Let's just look at a
couple of examples.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.680
So here here's an
inequality form LP.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:22.650
It says, minimize c transpose
x subject to a whole bunch

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.600
of linear inequalities.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:28.680
Then the dual function is well,
you just form the Lagrangian.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:33.660
The Lagrangian is going to
be c transpose x plus lambda

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.210
transpose Ax minus b.

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:39.120
And we rewrite that this way
so that we see it's an infimum.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.570
That's an affine function.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:43.050
And we know what the infimum
of an affine function is.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.820
It's usually minus infinity.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:49.370
But if the linear part of
the affine function vanishes,

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:51.370
then it's whatever
the constant is.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.050
So this is that.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.750
So the real dual
problem is this,

00:19:55.750 --> 00:20:00.070
is maximize this function
g subject to lambda

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.140
bigger than or equal to zero.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:07.360
That's the true or
literal dual problem.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:09.460
Then what we're
going to do is here

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:14.800
our objective is going to have
implicit or hidden constraints.

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.435
In this case, this
equality constraint.

00:20:18.435 --> 00:20:20.480
And if we make
that explicit, you

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.180
would write the dual
problem this way.

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:25.970
And that would be it.

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:27.110
So that's kind of cool.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:32.130
I mean what it says is
it says the following.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:35.390
It says that this linear
program is very closely

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:37.610
related to this one.

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.770
And we can say all
sorts of stuff about it.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:42.260
Number one, if you
have a lambda that's

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:44.750
feasible for this problem,
so it's non-negative,

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:46.520
satisfies this
equality constraint,

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:48.230
then this number
is a lower bound

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:51.290
on the optimal
value of that one.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:54.220
That's number one.

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:57.670
Number two, actually,
in this case,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.590
Slater's condition is
that this should hold.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:02.040
Well, the stronger form
of Slater's condition

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.790
says that if you have
linear inequalities,

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:05.190
you don't have to--

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:06.740
there is no requirement.

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:10.960
So in fact, this one
you have strong duality

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:15.775
all the time except in
extremely pathological cases.

00:21:15.775 --> 00:21:18.940
Where, in fact, you would have
d star minus infinity and p star

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:20.090
equals plus infinity.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:21.597
So they're both infeasible.

00:21:21.597 --> 00:21:22.930
And you have to construct these.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:24.880
They're extremely
small and silly.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:27.258
But at least now you
know that it can happen.

00:21:27.258 --> 00:21:28.300
It's not going to happen.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:36.480
And what's cool is if
you solve this problem,

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:39.180
there's actually a lambda,
which is feasible here

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:42.420
and this number is equal
to the objective there.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:45.630
And that gives you a
certificate of optimality,

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:46.830
which is awesome.

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:48.705
As a matter of fact,
what we're going to find

00:21:48.705 --> 00:21:51.180
is when you solve this
one by most methods,

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:54.270
whether you like it or not, it
is going to give you a lambda

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:54.910
here.

00:21:54.910 --> 00:22:00.390
So that's really cool because
it says when you solve an LP,

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:03.330
it comes back and it
says, yeah, OK, here's x.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.910
You check it's feasible and
you see its optimal value.

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.820
Or sorry, you see its value.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:13.710
It also returns a completely
independent checkable proof

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.560
that no feasible
point could have

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:17.790
an objective value smaller.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.260
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:20.910
This is super strong.

00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:23.610
In numerical computing,
this is very rare

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.770
to get something like that.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.080
But that's actually
what all solvers do.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:28.890
It's kind of cool.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:33.180
They return both what's
called a primal optimal

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:34.350
and a dual optimal.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:39.470
And the dual optimal serves as
a certificate of optimality.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.800
So these are very cool things.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:45.430
Let's look at another example.

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:47.280
Here's a quadratic program.

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:49.560
So here's the primal problem.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:51.780
So you minimize a quadratic
over a polyhedron.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:53.460
And we work out
the dual function

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:56.820
and it looks like this.

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:58.650
And so the dual problem is this.

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:02.310
And what it says is that
this problem and this problem

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:03.600
are intimately related.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:07.440
This says, any feasible
lambda here, this number,

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:10.110
is a lower bound on the
optimal value of that.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:11.940
So that's what this says.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.730
Oh, by the way,
any feasible point

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.750
here is an upper bound
on the optimal value

00:23:16.750 --> 00:23:18.540
of-- the objective
of any feasible

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:21.180
point here is an upper bound
on the optimal value of this.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:23.370
I mean, of course.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:27.870
So in this case, you
don't even need-- again,

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.500
these are linear the
strong Slater says,

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:34.050
you are excused from
checking Slater's constraint

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:36.600
for linear inequality
constraints.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.940
And in this problem,
there are no exceptions.

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:40.245
It's just p star equals d star.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:46.770
So this is the idea.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.470
By the way, this is a
stunning place to get to.

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:52.350
Because if you remember how
the whole story started,

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:55.800
it started very implausibly.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.390
Here's how this
whole thing started.

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:58.560
The whole thing
started this way.

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:00.393
You say, well, I have
inequality constraints

00:24:00.393 --> 00:24:01.920
and so here's the semantics.

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:03.210
Here's f1 of x.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:08.190
It's 0 if you are less
than or equal to 0 and plus

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:09.690
infinity if you're positive.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.690
So we said, that's
the problem I have.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.095
And you go, oh, cool.

00:24:14.095 --> 00:24:14.970
May I approximate it?

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:16.620
And you go, yeah,
go ahead, please.

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:18.537
And you say, well, I'm
going to approximate it

00:24:18.537 --> 00:24:21.027
with something that
looks like that.

00:24:21.027 --> 00:24:22.860
Then you look at that
and you're like, dude,

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:24.152
that's not a good approximate--

00:24:24.152 --> 00:24:26.130
I mean, that's not a
good approximation.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:27.360
I mean, it's not--

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.460
If someone had drawn something
that looked like this,

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:32.940
you would say, OK,
that's an approximation.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:35.250
But I don't know how
anyone could ever say

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:36.580
that this is an approximation.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:39.840
So if you remember, this
was the start of the story.

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:43.080
And it is deeply implausible.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:44.460
And here's what this is.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:48.270
What this says is that
weirdly, if you adjust

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:53.370
this correctly,
then very weirdly,

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:54.678
you will actually kind of get--

00:24:54.678 --> 00:24:56.220
I don't want to say
get the solution,

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:00.740
but you'll get the optimal
value of the original problem.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:04.070
So in other words, it looks
dumb but it turns out actually,

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:06.430
at least for convex
problems, it's not as dumb

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:08.825
as it might appear at first.

00:25:08.825 --> 00:25:09.760
Everybody got that?

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.390
So I don't know.

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:13.285
I find it interesting and cool.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.300
Oh, actually I think I'm going
to skip this and just say

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:21.680
the following.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:27.420
There are a number of
non-convex problems

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:32.580
where people know that
strong duality holds.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:35.430
And a famous one, we don't
have to worry about this,

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:40.320
is actually when you
minimize a non-quadratic--

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:44.340
sorry, a non convex quadratic
subject over, let's say,

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.020
a unit ball.

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:52.638
So turns out, in that case, and
it is not easy to show this,

00:25:52.638 --> 00:25:54.690
you have strong duality.

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:58.300
And that's actually super
interesting because--

00:25:58.300 --> 00:25:58.800
anyway.

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:01.740
So by the way, this is
actually used by some,

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:04.590
I think, kind of
weird people to argue

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:06.190
something, which I'll explain.

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:07.620
So the general idea is this.

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:10.740
The implication of this
is that any optimization

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:15.210
problem with two
quadratics, convex or not,

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.010
we can solve tractably.

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.120
Everybody got that?

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.160
Now you know a bunch
of them, right?

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.380
Eigenvalue problems,
singular value problems.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:23.620
If I walk up to you
on the street and say,

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:24.703
I have a symmetric matrix.

00:26:24.703 --> 00:26:28.840
Please maximize x transpose
Ax subject to x transpose

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:31.870
x equals 1, you can solve that.

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:33.610
That's an eigenvalue problem.

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.625
And you say, is that
an approximate solution

00:26:35.625 --> 00:26:36.250
or a heuristic?

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:37.910
And you'll know that's
the global solution.

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:39.577
And they go, oh, is
your problem convex?

00:26:39.577 --> 00:26:41.195
And you go, actually, it's not.

00:26:41.195 --> 00:26:42.445
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:48.020
So I mean it's OK.

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:48.730
It's all right.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:52.810
So there are people who
say that any problem you

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:56.140
can solve, anywhere
in optimization

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:58.060
is because it's convex.

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:00.640
That's a very strong
statement and it's weird,

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:02.570
but it's also partly true.

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:05.440
And so you start
asking questions like,

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:07.503
hey, in my computer
science class,

00:27:07.503 --> 00:27:08.920
in my baby computer
science class,

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:11.200
I found out that we
can tractably find

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:14.060
the shortest path in a graph.

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:14.560
Well, guess?

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:17.410
What it turns out that
the relaxation of that

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:19.840
can be proved to be tight.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:21.423
So there's zero
duality gap for that.

00:27:21.423 --> 00:27:23.590
I mean, I think it's silly
because it doesn't really

00:27:23.590 --> 00:27:24.423
do anything for you.

00:27:24.423 --> 00:27:26.560
You go, oh, should
I fire up like CVXPy

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.030
and use a linear
programming solver

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:28.840
to find the shortest path?

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:29.690
You're like, no.

00:27:29.690 --> 00:27:32.290
You should use the shortest path
algorithm, of which they have

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:33.430
a lot and they're really good.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:34.513
Use Dijkstra or something.

00:27:34.513 --> 00:27:36.550
Everybody following this?

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:41.320
Then they say, yeah, the other
problem is I can solve it.

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:44.020
I can solve these extremal
problems with matrices.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:46.930
I can solve eigen problems.

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:50.350
And then all this says
is you can solve problems

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:54.550
because the dual of your
eigen problem is A convex

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:57.400
and B has strong duality holds.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:58.340
Everybody see this?

00:27:58.340 --> 00:27:59.290
So it's a stretch.

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:01.180
But technically, it's true.

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:06.550
So it's an extreme point of
view but it's not a bad one.

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:10.990
I can't think of any problem in
any applied mathematics field

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:12.760
you can actually globally solve.

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:17.260
Where somehow secretly
behind the scenes convexity

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:18.848
is not lurking.

00:28:18.848 --> 00:28:20.140
Maybe it's one of these things.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:21.822
Anyway, so it's
just good to know.

00:28:21.822 --> 00:28:24.280
The two quadratics thing, that's
a very good thing to know.

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.200
Very few people know it.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:29.500
It is rediscovered
approximately every 10 years

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:31.640
in each of 10 different fields.

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:34.960
So as we speak,
someone is figuring out

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:37.360
that they can solve this
problem writing a hideous paper

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:39.790
with terrible notation
that's 25 pages

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:42.880
and think that in
their field, they just

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:44.620
found some deep thing.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:45.970
And it just keeps happening.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:49.520
There's even a paper
written on the rediscovery

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:53.090
of this fact, a history of it.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:54.500
So I mean it's actually sort--

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:56.970
But it is a really
good thing to know.

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:00.290
And it is not impossible that
you will bump into it someday.

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:04.130
There are also even weird,
sick, sick, sick things

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.290
that also have zero
duality gap and they come

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:08.570
from weird deep things in math.

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:12.650
There's one where you have
instead of a quadratic,

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.890
you could have a
quartic in six variables

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:18.530
over the complex field.

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:20.960
That would actually
have zero duality gap.

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:24.080
And the reason would go
back to some very deep thing

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:27.000
about whatever geometry
and stuff like that.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:30.030
It's algebraic geometry
and you're like, OK, fine.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:33.800
So there are isolated cases
where non-convex problems

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:35.588
have zero gap.

00:29:35.588 --> 00:29:36.380
That was all weird.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:40.190
And not only can you
forget that, I'd even

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:42.680
suggest forgetting what
I just said so just

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:45.890
because it's just weird.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:49.532
So now we're going to do a
geometric interpretation.

00:29:49.532 --> 00:29:50.990
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:53.960
going to do we're going to solve
a problem where you minimize

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:56.690
f0 of x subject to
f1 of x less than 0.

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:58.610
This is a non-convex
problem and we're just

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:02.660
going to-- this is a way
to visualize what p star

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:05.360
and d star are and all
that kind of stuff.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:09.320
So what G is-- so G is
going to be the set.

00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:11.570
Remember, x could be in
dimension 100, doesn't matter.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:15.470
But G is the set of pairs
of constraint function

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:17.240
and objective.

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:18.922
So that's in a plane,
we can plot that,

00:30:18.922 --> 00:30:20.130
and that's what we have here.

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:24.590
So if you imagine going
over all possible x's you

00:30:24.590 --> 00:30:29.360
get this blob which is G. And
then let's talk about that.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:36.830
Well, this axis is f1, which
is your constraint function.

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:40.940
And basically, the semantics
is, everything to the left

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:43.040
is acceptable and
everything to the right

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:44.620
is completely unacceptable.

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:47.400
So all the x's
corresponding all of this,

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:48.690
completely unacceptable.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:51.432
These are cool.

00:30:51.432 --> 00:30:52.890
So that's what the
constraint says.

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:54.348
It says ignore the
right hand side.

00:30:54.348 --> 00:30:56.980
Or sorry, you're not allowed
to look at the right hand side.

00:30:56.980 --> 00:31:01.620
I mean, you can look at it
but they are not candidates.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:06.330
Now, the vertical axis is
actually the objective.

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:08.910
And the semantics of
objective is extremely simple.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.260
The smaller it is, the better.

00:31:11.260 --> 00:31:15.100
And so graphically now, I can
tell you precisely what to do.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:17.100
It says when you see
one of these blobs,

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.640
you ignore everything
on the right.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:22.410
And you can attach a comment to
it saying they're infeasible.

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:25.170
So you can only focus on
this thing, this part.

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:27.060
And then there your
goal is to find

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:30.250
the thing that is lowest, has
the lowest potential energy,

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:31.470
let's say.

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:34.020
And that would be
this thing here.

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:35.530
Everyone got that?

00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:37.890
And so if we go over
here, we'll plot

00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:41.370
p star on this axis
and that's p star.

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:42.390
Makes sense, right?

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:43.530
So that's the picture.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:49.020
Now let's look at
the dual function,

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:51.000
which is actually interesting.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:56.330
So let's look at the Lagrangian,
by the way, is a function--

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:59.090
of course it's a function of x,
which has dimension 100, which

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:00.530
I just made up.

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:03.170
But the point is,
the Lagrangian only

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:06.620
depends on two numbers,
which is f1 of x and f0 of x.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:09.080
And in fact, it's
silly, it's just

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:11.600
f0 of x plus lambda f1 of x.

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:13.220
That's the Lagrangian.

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:16.700
So I can plot the level
curves of the Lagrangian

00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:17.990
and they look like.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:20.660
These are the level curves
of the Lagrangian here.

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:23.390
And I think the slope is--

00:32:23.390 --> 00:32:24.290
let's see.

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:27.920
The slope is maybe minus
lambda or something like that?

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:28.790
I think that's it.

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:30.020
Or minus 1 over lambda.

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:31.700
Maybe minus lambda.

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:36.692
So this is the level-- this
is for one value of lambda.

00:32:36.692 --> 00:32:38.780
This is a level curve
of the Lagrangian.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:41.450
And they would look like this.

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:45.570
And so your goal is when
someone fixes lambda, which

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:50.220
is the slope, your goal is to
go as far as possible this way

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:55.490
until you're still touching
G. Everybody got that?

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:57.740
And so you can see
I've plotted it

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:01.310
for what happens for
different values of lambda.

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:03.408
And what you can see
what happens here,

00:33:03.408 --> 00:33:05.450
it turns out when you take
this line when it just

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:08.420
touches, where it
intersects this axis,

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:10.710
actually this is g of lambda.

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:11.460
And look at that.

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:14.220
Sure enough, g of lambda
is bigger is less than

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:15.060
or equal to p star.

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:15.602
We knew that.

00:33:15.602 --> 00:33:17.190
It's a lower bound.

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:21.660
And now I can picture in my
head fiddling with lambda.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:26.380
If I made lambda
super big, let's say--

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:29.980
well, let me first
figure out what it is.

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:30.480
Sorry.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:33.750
If lambda is super big, that
means that the slope here

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:36.570
is extremely small.

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:38.250
And then you can actually see--

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:40.620
in fact, as lambda
goes, you're going

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:44.310
to get-- you're going to get
a value around here, d star.

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:46.050
Sorry, that's d.

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:50.100
Now, as I change the slope like
this, what's going to happen

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:53.970
is this intersection point
is going to move up here.

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:56.430
Right when it's just
touching these two points,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:57.750
it's going to hit d star.

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:00.540
And when I increase the
slope again, what happens

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.038
is my point of
contact that's here

00:34:02.038 --> 00:34:04.080
it's going to rotate around
that point of contact

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:06.060
and start going down again.

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:09.989
And I just, at least, visually
traced out what G looks like.

00:34:09.989 --> 00:34:13.318
It goes up, hits a
maximum, then goes down.

00:34:13.318 --> 00:34:14.110
Well, it's concave.

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:14.949
We knew that.

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:16.810
Everybody got this?

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:21.139
And now for this
problem, that's the gap.

00:34:21.139 --> 00:34:23.889
That's the gap, is
this thing here.

00:34:23.889 --> 00:34:26.850
It's p star minus d star.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:29.219
And now you actually
see why you have a gap.

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:33.510
You have a gap because
there's a dent in this set G,

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:35.280
meaning a non-convex portion.

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:38.280
So if G were convex,
you wouldn't have

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:39.872
that-- you wouldn't have this.

00:34:39.872 --> 00:34:41.080
Everyone see what I'm saying?

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:44.280
So this picture,
once you internalize,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.190
you can kind of see why it
is that for convex problems,

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:48.719
there's zero duality gap.

00:34:48.719 --> 00:34:52.650
And roughly speaking,
what it says is that--

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:53.275
it's a surface.

00:34:53.275 --> 00:34:55.150
It's going to not be
exactly G But it's going

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:56.280
to be closely related to g.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.368
It says that set is convex.

00:34:58.368 --> 00:34:59.910
And there's going
to be-- what you're

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:01.830
going to do is you're
going to be able to get

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.350
a hyperplane right up to it
to any point on that surface

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.350
and that's what's going to allow
you to get zero duality gap.

00:35:08.350 --> 00:35:11.620
Again, this is just
for you to understand

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:13.000
why all this stuff works.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.310
That's the geometric
interpretation.

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:22.300
So to actually make this
official, what you do

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:28.270
is in this setting, we
have f1 and we have f0.

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:30.640
f1 we want to be less
than or equal to 0.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.440
And f0 we'll take as
small as it can go.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:36.050
So if I form the
epigraph of that.

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:37.630
So if I take all
the points, that's

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:39.610
A is everything
that's like that,

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:42.940
it's actually going to be
the same thing because--

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:45.010
I mean, actually
these are just poin--

00:35:45.010 --> 00:35:47.320
You don't really care
about these points.

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:49.180
But it turns out
this set is going

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:53.380
to be-- this set A for a convex
problem is going to be convex.

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:57.200
I guess, that's obvious
because it's like a multi--

00:35:57.200 --> 00:35:59.320
actually it's the
intersection of two epigraphs

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:02.080
so it's going to be convex.

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:04.670
So this is the picture.

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:06.790
And then basically
what it says is--

00:36:06.790 --> 00:36:10.420
let me redraw this
for a convex set

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:12.370
and I'll show you
what it looks like.

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:14.140
But it should be obvious.

00:36:14.140 --> 00:36:16.750
So what happens
is you have this.

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:19.645
And for a convex set, this
thing might look like this

00:36:19.645 --> 00:36:20.770
or something, I don't know.

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:22.540
Let's pretend it
looks like that.

00:36:25.130 --> 00:36:27.650
So p star is here.

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:28.820
How do you get p star?

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:32.270
Well, I ignore what happens
over here, I look over here,

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:34.070
and I go down as low as I can.

00:36:34.070 --> 00:36:35.480
And this is p star.

00:36:38.380 --> 00:36:40.090
And now how do I get d star?

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:43.180
Well, that's simple.

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:44.500
Well, I like to think of this.

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:46.690
I'm going to put a line.

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:48.190
I think it's minus
1 over lambda.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:48.940
It doesn't matter.

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:51.100
I can change the slope on that.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:53.698
What I do with that line is
I have a point of contact

00:36:53.698 --> 00:36:55.990
because I'm really going as
far down as I can this way.

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:57.130
I have a point of contact.

00:36:57.130 --> 00:36:58.570
I change lambda.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:01.990
And this is g of lambda,
everybody got that?

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:03.640
Here, this intersection.

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:08.270
And now the question is how
big can I make g of lambda?

00:37:08.270 --> 00:37:11.800
Which, by the way,
has a name d star.

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:14.320
And so the answer
is you rotate this

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:18.190
and if I take this
value of lambda, that's

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:20.495
a particularly poor value
because this is going

00:37:20.495 --> 00:37:22.120
to eventually hit
this and you're going

00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:24.160
to have a very negative number.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:27.340
If I take lambda way down
here, that's a bad value too.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:31.210
And we can see clearly what's
actually going to do the trick.

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:34.420
It's going to be
really very cool.

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:40.600
What I do is right here I will
find the correct slope is,

00:37:40.600 --> 00:37:44.950
in fact, is just the tangent
to this at that point.

00:37:44.950 --> 00:37:48.160
And there, p star equals d star.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:49.330
Everybody got it?

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.820
So it's actually kind of cool.

00:37:52.820 --> 00:37:55.630
Also, this hints at
lots of other stuff.

00:37:55.630 --> 00:37:59.090
This hints at another topic
we're going to look at,

00:37:59.090 --> 00:38:00.940
which is this.

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.580
This says that suppose you
went back and negotiated

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:04.990
the constraint.

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:09.520
The current constraint is
f1 of x is less than 0.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:12.020
But suppose you went back
and you said, you know what?

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:18.790
I can't do it with a power
budget of 50 milliwatts.

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:20.258
It won't clock right.

00:38:20.258 --> 00:38:21.550
It's going to use too much air.

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:22.550
I don't know what it is.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:23.200
I can't do it.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:27.160
Please, give me 55
milliwatts as a budget.

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:31.795
That has the effect of
moving this over to here.

00:38:31.795 --> 00:38:33.310
Then let's say
that says you could

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:37.040
have this is less than something
else that's small right there.

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:39.910
That's my extra power.

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:42.490
Now to solve that
problem, you can

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:44.560
see that the objective
is going to go down.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:48.100
I mean, it had to.

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:49.690
You started with 50
milliwatt budget.

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:50.840
You solved the problem.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.180
And you went negotiated.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:56.330
And they said, sure, take 55.

00:38:56.330 --> 00:38:57.980
And you're like, OK, thanks.

00:38:57.980 --> 00:38:59.970
Your objective is
only going to go down.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:02.450
But here's the cool part,
you can see immediately

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:05.660
that the amount it
goes down is actually

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:08.780
extremely well approximated
by this Lagrange multiplier.

00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:09.810
Everybody got that?

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:11.643
So we're getting a
little ahead of ourselves

00:39:11.643 --> 00:39:12.570
but that's the idea.

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:16.010
So you can even see the optimal
Lagrange multiplier here

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:17.430
as a very cool--

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:17.930
That's?

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:19.640
Why people call it shadow price.

00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:23.300
It's basically how
much would you pay--

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:26.690
how much would you pay
in terms of the objective

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:30.128
to relax or restrict
that constraint.

00:39:30.128 --> 00:39:30.920
So that's the idea.

00:39:30.920 --> 00:39:33.805
Everybody got the idea there?

00:39:33.805 --> 00:39:35.180
So I mean these
are-- we're going

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:37.040
to look at this more
carefully in a minute

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:37.873
but that's the idea.

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:43.050
Oh, by the way,
also, with this, you

00:39:43.050 --> 00:39:47.790
can make up one of those sick
problems that's convex and has

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:49.320
positive duality gap.

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:52.290
I'm not going to do it
just because you shouldn't.

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:54.540
This is just one of those
things you shouldn't do.

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:57.840
You could do it but I'm
not going to because it's

00:39:57.840 --> 00:39:58.740
silly and all that.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:04.640
Marching right along,
we get to this idea

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:06.200
of complementary slackness.

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:10.070
So let's see what happens
if you have zero duality gap

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:11.390
and they're achieved.

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:17.630
So what that means is you find
an x here, if you have that.

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:19.190
This is a strong
duality so I have x.

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:22.280
And then lambda star, nu star
are optimal dual variables.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:26.030
By the way you would call
this optimal dual variables.

00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:30.260
And you'd call x star, comma,
lambda star, nu star, that's

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:32.042
primal dual optimal variables.

00:40:32.042 --> 00:40:33.500
So if you said
that, everyone would

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:36.680
know what you're talking about.

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:38.180
Well, I mean this
is what it means.

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:39.080
That's p star.

00:40:39.080 --> 00:40:40.490
That's d star.

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:42.350
But the definition
of this is it's

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:44.690
the infimum of the
Lagrangian over x

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:46.610
with the lambda stars in it.

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:47.690
That's fine.

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:50.240
But this is the
infimum over all x.

00:40:50.240 --> 00:40:53.480
Well, that's for sure less
than or equal to-- if I plug-in

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:55.550
x equals x star because
that's one of the things

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:57.380
you would infimize over.

00:40:57.380 --> 00:40:58.790
So you get this.

00:40:58.790 --> 00:41:00.780
And now let's take
a look at this.

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:04.680
x star is optimal,
so these are 0.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:07.690
The his are 0 because
that's the original problem.

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:09.430
So this goes away.

00:41:09.430 --> 00:41:12.240
These are less
than or equal to 0

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:15.320
because that's what it
means to be feasible.

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:17.080
The lambdas are
bigger and equal to 0.

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:20.155
And so this sum here is
less than or equal to 0.

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:26.250
So this thing is less
than or equal to that.

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:29.450
Oh, but wait a minute, now I
have a chain of these things

00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:34.700
and that says that these
are actually equalities.

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:36.830
Now let's see what that means.

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:39.150
We go back to this term and
you know what that means?

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:42.240
That means that this term is 0.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:43.980
Now let's talk about that term.

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:48.510
It's a sum of products of a
non-negative number, lambda i

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:52.950
star, times a non-positive
number, fi star.

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:58.068
And so actually each term in the
sum is less than or equal to 0.

00:41:58.068 --> 00:41:59.610
Now if I walk up to
you on the street

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:02.700
and I say I have a sum of things
each less than or equal to 0

00:42:02.700 --> 00:42:06.780
and the sum is 0,
what can you conclude?

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:08.880
What's that?

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:09.540
What?

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:11.970
Well, every single
one of them was 0

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:15.450
because if any one is
non-zero, not going to work.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:17.590
So everybody got this?

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:24.050
And that tells you that
lambda i star, fi star is 0.

00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:28.820
That's called complementary
slackness because when fi of x

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:32.420
is 0, we say that
constraint is tight.

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:38.340
If f i x is less than 0, we
say that constraint is slack.

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:40.620
It's good notation.

00:42:40.620 --> 00:42:43.110
This says if a constraint is--

00:42:43.110 --> 00:42:46.980
if a constraint is
slack, then for sure

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:51.260
the Lagrange
multiplier has to be 0.

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:52.160
That's what it says.

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:59.030
And you can work out
many ways to say it.

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:03.620
If this thing is non-zero,
then for sure have to be tight.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:08.120
So actually what's cool is the
connection to these, obviously

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:10.370
when you say slack
and tight, these

00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:13.340
are references to
mechanical analogs.

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:16.802
And in fact, for mechanical
analogs, it works perfectly.

00:43:16.802 --> 00:43:18.260
You can read in
the book about this

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:21.320
where you minimize the
potential energy of something

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:23.310
subject to constraints.

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:27.050
For example, if there's a
surface and something wants

00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:30.110
to go that way and it
hits it, then it turns out

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:36.230
literally slack means you're
not touching that boundary.

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:37.940
Tight means you're touching it.

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:39.890
And then the coolest
thing ever happens.

00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:46.430
It turns out lambda is literally
the contact force in newtons.

00:43:46.430 --> 00:43:48.440
So it says if you're
not touching--

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:50.090
or let's take a chain.

00:43:50.090 --> 00:43:54.275
Let's say I have I have two
things connected by a cable.

00:43:54.275 --> 00:43:55.275
It's a mechanical thing.

00:43:55.275 --> 00:43:57.820
I minimize potential energy
and blah, blah, blah.

00:43:57.820 --> 00:43:59.362
And there it's all
hanging-- now it's

00:43:59.362 --> 00:44:01.590
all hanging in some
nice configuration.

00:44:01.590 --> 00:44:04.620
What it says is if
a cable is slack,

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:08.010
their lambda is precisely
the tension in the cable.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.880
If it's slack,
there's no tension.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:13.920
Some will actually be not slack.

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:15.090
They'll be tight.

00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:17.400
Or in that case,
you would say taut.

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:20.770
And then the tension
will be precisely lambda.

00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:23.080
By the way, I'll be
able to show that when

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:25.990
we do a little bit
later today, that's

00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:29.410
literally the tension or the
contact force in newtons.

00:44:29.410 --> 00:44:30.888
We'll get to that shortly.

00:44:30.888 --> 00:44:32.680
I mean if you just know
a little bit, which

00:44:32.680 --> 00:44:33.638
is actually all I know.

00:44:33.638 --> 00:44:34.450
Yes?

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:38.160
Here how do we know that lambda
star and fi star are finite?

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:40.830
Can't it be the case that when
we solve the dual problem,

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:42.900
we see that it goes to infinity?

00:44:42.900 --> 00:44:47.820
So yes, this is the
case where x star

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:51.540
is prime-- there exists a
primal optimum and dual optimum.

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:56.352
You're talking about cases where
it's an infeasibility problem

00:44:56.352 --> 00:44:57.310
or something like that.

00:44:57.310 --> 00:44:59.160
In which case, you
don't have this.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:01.605
The dual is unbounded
or something like that.

00:45:01.605 --> 00:45:03.450
So this is not that case.

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:06.030
That case does come up.

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:09.510
So in fact, I'll say something
about that case a little bit

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:10.180
later.

00:45:10.180 --> 00:45:13.360
So everybody got this so far?

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:16.020
It's actually all very--
it's all pretty cool right.

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:21.450
Now we march on to
something called

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:24.030
the KKT, or Karush-Kuhn-Tucker.

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:25.530
This is actually a
very sweet story,

00:45:25.530 --> 00:45:32.520
that Lagrange and others
in the early 19th century

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:36.210
came up with these things
that have their names on them.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:39.720
These conditions for
minimizing a function subject

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:42.780
to some equality constraints.

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:47.580
And that's how Lagrange got
his name on these things.

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:50.440
And they did it for like one
or two and stuff like that.

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:53.100
And so it's fine.

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:59.430
The extension to
inequalities, actually--

00:45:59.430 --> 00:46:03.390
I mean certainly if you'd asked
a mid to late 19th century

00:46:03.390 --> 00:46:06.023
mathematician to figure
out how does it--

00:46:06.023 --> 00:46:07.440
they would have
come up with this.

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:09.900
But for whatever
reason, it wasn't until,

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:12.240
I don't know, like
1951 that some people

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:15.270
at Princeton or whatever,
that's like Kuhn and Tucker

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:16.920
came up with these.

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:18.300
And I mean it's OK.

00:46:18.300 --> 00:46:20.220
And they were like,
wow, that's cool.

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:22.122
It's an extension of
what Lagrange did.

00:46:22.122 --> 00:46:22.830
And I don't know.

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:24.570
It makes me feel
good and all that.

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:26.310
Everything's cool.

00:46:26.310 --> 00:46:28.600
They became called the
Kuhn-Tucker conditions.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:30.090
So if you look in--

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:30.960
I don't know.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:36.540
If you look in papers of a
certain era, maybe from 1960

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:41.640
or '65, you will literally see
just Kuhn-Tucker conditions.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:44.700
Anyway, they then later
discovered after a decade

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:49.200
or so, they discovered
a 1938 master's thesis

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:52.810
by Karush that everything
in it, like everything--

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:53.310
Unpublished.

00:46:53.310 --> 00:46:54.600
Everything was in it.

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:56.380
So then actually they
did the right thing,

00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:58.102
which is to then
popularize that it

00:46:58.102 --> 00:46:59.310
should be Karush-Kuhn-Tucker.

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:02.460
But actually, the real story
is that you should never

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:05.250
ever pretend that you
did something first

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:08.620
because number one, almost
certainly Gauss knew about it.

00:47:08.620 --> 00:47:09.850
We could start with that.

00:47:09.850 --> 00:47:10.930
For sure.

00:47:10.930 --> 00:47:15.340
And then I mean,
then I don't know

00:47:15.340 --> 00:47:19.100
I have friends who remind
me that no, this is not

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:20.350
the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.

00:47:20.350 --> 00:47:22.270
It's the Bhattacharya
inequality,

00:47:22.270 --> 00:47:24.340
which is 500 years earlier.

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:27.383
So anyway.

00:47:27.383 --> 00:47:29.050
You should also not
hang out with people

00:47:29.050 --> 00:47:30.250
who say things like that.

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:30.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:30.940 --> 00:47:33.490
Like, I discovered
something new.

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:35.410
Because it's extremely unlikely.

00:47:35.410 --> 00:47:37.990
All I can say is every time I
ever looked into an allegation

00:47:37.990 --> 00:47:40.750
like that, they proved false.

00:47:40.750 --> 00:47:42.310
Including, by the way, myself.

00:47:42.310 --> 00:47:45.790
So it's totally fine.

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:46.720
So anyway, sorry.

00:47:46.720 --> 00:47:50.670
That was just the story
behind this, the name.

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:51.580
So here they are.

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:53.040
These are the KKT conditions.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:57.050
And they are, it says--

00:47:57.050 --> 00:47:59.795
Look, this is a problem, I have
differentiable functions here.

00:47:59.795 --> 00:48:00.920
That's where it's normally.

00:48:00.920 --> 00:48:02.180
They are extensions
to non-differentiable,

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:02.805
doesn't matter.

00:48:02.805 --> 00:48:04.550
But here's the way it works.

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:07.610
And these are going to
end up being conditions

00:48:07.610 --> 00:48:09.830
that for convex
problems are actually

00:48:09.830 --> 00:48:11.970
the optimality conditions.

00:48:11.970 --> 00:48:13.220
But in general, here they are.

00:48:13.220 --> 00:48:15.500
It says, well, look,
if you want to be

00:48:15.500 --> 00:48:18.530
a candidate for being
optimal, you better

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:20.390
satisfy the constraints.

00:48:20.390 --> 00:48:23.000
Otherwise, you're
not a candidate.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:27.110
So the first one is you
have to be feasible.

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:28.700
Oh, and by the
way what I'm doing

00:48:28.700 --> 00:48:30.470
is we're actually looking for--

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:34.610
its conditions that
couple x, lambda, and nu.

00:48:34.610 --> 00:48:37.970
The other one is,
well, look, I mean,

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:40.310
if the lambda has entries
that are negative,

00:48:40.310 --> 00:48:41.810
it doesn't make any
sense because it

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:45.590
means you're basically
being incented and paid

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:47.660
to violate a constraint.

00:48:47.660 --> 00:48:49.460
And that's why it
doesn't make any sense

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:52.460
to have lambda i less than
0-- any of the lambda i's

00:48:52.460 --> 00:48:54.470
negative, OK?

00:48:54.470 --> 00:48:55.500
That's this one.

00:48:55.500 --> 00:48:57.780
And then finally you have
complementary slackness.

00:48:57.780 --> 00:49:02.280
That's this innocent
thing that says

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:05.790
you can have a positive Lagrange
multiplier for an inequality

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:09.730
constraint if and
only if you're tight.

00:49:09.730 --> 00:49:11.880
That's what this says.

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:15.180
And then the final
one is this is,

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:17.910
the gradient of the Lagrangian
with respect to x vanishes.

00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:20.400
And that's just this condition.

00:49:20.400 --> 00:49:24.540
This is basically the gradient
sub x of the Lagrangian

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:26.010
and that's this.

00:49:26.010 --> 00:49:28.170
And so I mean, this
very much puts it

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:31.650
in the style of calculus.

00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:34.530
This extends the following.

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:38.100
If you want to minimize
a convex function f0,

00:49:38.100 --> 00:49:41.850
then the optimality condition
is the gradient of f0

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:45.360
is equal to 0, period,
if it's differentiable.

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:46.470
That's just the opt--

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:49.350
And that's actually
necessary and sufficient.

00:49:49.350 --> 00:49:53.430
So this is generalizing
that to include constraints.

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:54.330
So this is.

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:57.780
And what we've seen is if
you have a convex problem

00:49:57.780 --> 00:49:59.670
and you have strong duality--

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:02.760
actually it turns out if you
have any problem even if it's

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:06.360
not convex, if
strong duality holds

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:09.030
and you obtain the x star,
lambda star, nu star,

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.220
then these KKT conditions hold.

00:50:13.890 --> 00:50:18.270
And you refer to in a
problem, if x, lambda,

00:50:18.270 --> 00:50:21.000
nu satisfy the KKT
conditions, that's

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:22.890
referred to as a KKT point.

00:50:22.890 --> 00:50:24.780
That's all it means.

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:27.000
And then in the
usual non-convex case

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:28.590
someone says, is it optimal?

00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:29.820
And you go, might be.

00:50:29.820 --> 00:50:30.810
Yeah, sure.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:34.320
I mean it's like, look, it's the
same thing as if someone says

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:38.260
minimize a non-convex
function and you go,

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:39.848
I'll look at gradient equals 0.

00:50:39.848 --> 00:50:41.890
And you find a point where
gradient is equal to 0

00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:44.830
and someone says, does that
minimize your function?

00:50:44.830 --> 00:50:48.160
And the correct and legal
answer is, I don't know.

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:48.940
Maybe.

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:50.350
Could.

00:50:50.350 --> 00:50:51.370
Possibly.

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:53.440
And so then they have a
different word for it.

00:50:53.440 --> 00:50:56.990
They call it a stationary
point or something like that.

00:50:56.990 --> 00:50:58.930
And maybe there's only
five stationary points.

00:50:58.930 --> 00:51:01.570
Then one of them is
going to be the best.

00:51:01.570 --> 00:51:03.265
Anyway, I won't go on.

00:51:03.265 --> 00:51:06.730
So this is KKT conditions.

00:51:06.730 --> 00:51:10.270
Now for a convex
problem, this is cool.

00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:12.580
If you satisfy the
KKT conditions,

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:14.650
then you're optimal.

00:51:14.650 --> 00:51:18.050
And I mean it's
actually really cool.

00:51:18.050 --> 00:51:21.550
It basically says that
complementary slackness

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:24.265
means that when you look back
at the Lagrangian, all those fi

00:51:24.265 --> 00:51:26.560
and lambda i's, they're all 0.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:30.370
So in fact, what it says
is, your objective value

00:51:30.370 --> 00:51:33.340
for these three things, the
objective value of x tilde

00:51:33.340 --> 00:51:36.190
is also the Lagrangian.

00:51:36.190 --> 00:51:39.010
Now the Lagrangian
for a convex problem

00:51:39.010 --> 00:51:42.160
is convex because it's
f0, which is convex.

00:51:42.160 --> 00:51:43.540
Plus some lambda i.

00:51:43.540 --> 00:51:45.910
Lambda i are non-negative,
very important,

00:51:45.910 --> 00:51:49.540
times fi that's convex.

00:51:49.540 --> 00:51:52.540
And then the last one
is plus nu transpose

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:56.260
and then some affine functions.

00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:58.660
That's also convex.

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:01.960
But if the gradient vanishes
for a convex problem,

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:04.300
it's a minimizer.

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:08.920
So that says that when you
minimize the Lagrangian,

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:10.270
you actually get this.

00:52:10.270 --> 00:52:11.800
But then that means this holds.

00:52:11.800 --> 00:52:16.460
But that's the primal objective
and the dual objective.

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:20.360
If they're equal,
then end of story.

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:22.790
x tilde is primal optimal.

00:52:22.790 --> 00:52:27.400
And lambda star, nu
star are dual optimal.

00:52:27.400 --> 00:52:28.420
Everybody got this?

00:52:30.940 --> 00:52:33.250
And you could get into stuff
with Slater's condition.

00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:34.510
I actually don't want to.

00:52:34.510 --> 00:52:36.550
I think probably the
best way to remember

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:39.490
this is simply KKT conditions
are a set of conditions that

00:52:39.490 --> 00:52:41.770
generalize the silly stuff
from calculus-- well,

00:52:41.770 --> 00:52:43.540
they generalize
gradient vanishing

00:52:43.540 --> 00:52:47.170
as the optimality condition
for a convex function.

00:52:47.170 --> 00:52:51.280
It generalizes the 1830
Lagrange multiplier thing.

00:52:51.280 --> 00:52:54.250
So it says that the thing
you were taught mindlessly

00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:57.610
in your calculus class, which
is form this as Lagrangian,

00:52:57.610 --> 00:52:59.860
take the partial with respect
to x so that equal to 0,

00:52:59.860 --> 00:53:01.330
take the partial derivative
with respect to the Lagrange

00:53:01.330 --> 00:53:01.660
multiplier.

00:53:01.660 --> 00:53:02.830
We'll set that equal to 0.

00:53:02.830 --> 00:53:04.887
And you'd say, OK,
cool, I did that.

00:53:04.887 --> 00:53:06.220
And you go, that's the solution.

00:53:06.220 --> 00:53:09.370
Anyway, it generalizes
that and now

00:53:09.370 --> 00:53:11.680
it handles inequality
constraints.

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:17.470
So probably that's the best way
to think of the KKT conditions.

00:53:17.470 --> 00:53:20.440
So there's a handful of
problems where you can actually

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:22.090
solve things analytically.

00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:24.280
I'll just quickly
go over one example.

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:27.040
It's a famous one
from communications.

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:30.202
And let me just give a little
background on the problem.

00:53:30.202 --> 00:53:31.660
So the background
on the problem is

00:53:31.660 --> 00:53:33.850
that I have a wireless device.

00:53:33.850 --> 00:53:36.460
In fact, it's actually happening
all around us right now.

00:53:36.460 --> 00:53:38.200
So you have a wireless device.

00:53:38.200 --> 00:53:40.510
What I do is I
want to communicate

00:53:40.510 --> 00:53:42.520
to another terminal over there.

00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:46.340
I divide that into 512 channels.

00:53:46.340 --> 00:53:49.250
Each channel will have its
own signal to noise ratio

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:50.960
because other people
are transmitting

00:53:50.960 --> 00:53:53.270
and other stuff is
going down like that.

00:53:53.270 --> 00:53:54.740
That's the signal
to noise ratio.

00:53:54.740 --> 00:53:57.320
Then the question is,
which of those channels

00:53:57.320 --> 00:53:59.900
should I transmit on
and at what powers

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:02.120
and to maximize my bit rate.

00:54:02.120 --> 00:54:04.160
Again, if you're not
into communications,

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:06.420
you don't need to know this.

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:11.840
And so this is going to be
the log of xi plus alpha.

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:14.780
xi is going to be like the
power I put into channel i.

00:54:14.780 --> 00:54:17.675
And this is actually going
to be-- with some constants

00:54:17.675 --> 00:54:19.550
out in front, it's going
to be the bandwidth.

00:54:19.550 --> 00:54:20.967
It's the number
of bits per second

00:54:20.967 --> 00:54:22.520
I can pump down channel i.

00:54:22.520 --> 00:54:24.110
Again, if you know--

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:26.660
how many people here have
taken information theory,

00:54:26.660 --> 00:54:29.960
or communication class,
or anything like that?

00:54:29.960 --> 00:54:31.640
That's all, OK.

00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:32.360
It's cool.

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:34.280
Fine.

00:54:34.280 --> 00:54:35.120
That's it.

00:54:35.120 --> 00:54:37.310
So this basically
says figure out

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:39.920
the channel the powers to
transmit on all your channels

00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:43.370
to maximize your
total bandwidth.

00:54:43.370 --> 00:54:47.180
And anyway, so here you
write down the KKT conditions

00:54:47.180 --> 00:54:49.640
and they actually
look like this.

00:54:49.640 --> 00:54:54.170
And this is one of those
extremely few problems where

00:54:54.170 --> 00:54:56.810
you can actually just sit
out, get out your 19th century

00:54:56.810 --> 00:55:01.460
pencil and paper, and actually
just solve the KKT conditions.

00:55:01.460 --> 00:55:04.940
So I mean actually,
I won't go into that,

00:55:04.940 --> 00:55:08.450
except I'll give you
the interpretation.

00:55:08.450 --> 00:55:10.620
People figured this out
in the '70s or something.

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:12.120
And they were like,
this is awesome.

00:55:12.120 --> 00:55:14.270
And it's actually kind of
silly because even armed

00:55:14.270 --> 00:55:16.520
with the analytical
formula, a numerical method

00:55:16.520 --> 00:55:19.380
will compute it just as fast.

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:21.830
Not only that, you can add
other constraints to it

00:55:21.830 --> 00:55:24.560
and stuff like that and
it'll still be just as fast.

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:27.500
Anyway, so people
who celebrate these--

00:55:27.500 --> 00:55:30.090
I mean, look it's nice to
have an analytical formula

00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:34.200
for something but it is kind
of silly in this day and age.

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:43.320
So here the idea is
these are the alphas.

00:55:43.320 --> 00:55:46.110
So if alpha is big,
it means that there's

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:48.930
a lot of noise on that channel.

00:55:48.930 --> 00:55:52.680
And then what it
says is this height,

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:57.270
the dark height is actually the
power you should transmit at.

00:55:57.270 --> 00:56:00.180
And then what it does is
it says flood this thing

00:56:00.180 --> 00:56:02.850
to a certain level
until you have one unit

00:56:02.850 --> 00:56:03.690
or whatever this is.

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:06.930
One unit of water in here.

00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:09.180
When you do, here's
what you'll find out.

00:56:09.180 --> 00:56:12.353
This channel, it's
got a lot of noise

00:56:12.353 --> 00:56:14.520
and you should just put a
little bit of power on it.

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:19.290
This channel is so bad you
should put no power on it.

00:56:19.290 --> 00:56:21.105
We knew we were going
to use this one.

00:56:21.105 --> 00:56:23.730
That's the cleanest channel you
have and you put a lot of power

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:24.230
in it.

00:56:24.230 --> 00:56:26.157
Anyway, it's fine.

00:56:26.157 --> 00:56:27.990
For the people who
raised their hand before,

00:56:27.990 --> 00:56:30.210
did you see water
filling in that class?

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:31.620
You did.

00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:33.090
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:35.790
Did they know it was
a convex problem?

00:56:35.790 --> 00:56:36.600
Yeah, I think so.

00:56:36.600 --> 00:56:37.100
They did?

00:56:37.100 --> 00:56:37.740
OK, good.

00:56:37.740 --> 00:56:38.940
OK, that's progress.

00:56:38.940 --> 00:56:39.870
Good.

00:56:39.870 --> 00:56:41.815
Good for them.

00:56:41.815 --> 00:56:42.690
These are my friends.

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:43.940
Where was that?

00:56:43.940 --> 00:56:45.390
[INAUDIBLE] Caltech.

00:56:45.390 --> 00:56:46.243
At Caltech.

00:56:46.243 --> 00:56:47.160
Yeah, they would know.

00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:47.890
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:47.890 --> 00:56:48.160
Fine.

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:48.660
That's fine.

00:56:48.660 --> 00:56:49.950
OK, good.

00:56:49.950 --> 00:56:51.780
Fine.

00:56:51.780 --> 00:56:56.130
Now we get to a very cool,
and incredibly practical,

00:56:56.130 --> 00:56:59.655
and useful application
of duality right.

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:05.700
So here it is.

00:57:05.700 --> 00:57:08.430
Here's your original
problem, like this.

00:57:08.430 --> 00:57:09.990
And now what we're
going to do is

00:57:09.990 --> 00:57:14.130
we're going to imagine
perturbing the constraints.

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:16.410
Perturbing the constraints
means we're going

00:57:16.410 --> 00:57:17.830
to change the right hand side.

00:57:17.830 --> 00:57:22.650
So here I'm going to put a ui
for the inequality constraints

00:57:22.650 --> 00:57:24.560
and a vi for these.

00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:25.860
Super interesting.

00:57:25.860 --> 00:57:28.490
So let me say-- and there's
lots of language people

00:57:28.490 --> 00:57:30.350
would use to describe this.

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:37.800
If ui is positive, it says you
have relaxed the constraint.

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:38.850
Everyone understand that?

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:41.610
Because you relaxed it
because you made it easier--

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:43.800
more xs are satisfied.

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:45.660
So basically that's
what happened when

00:57:45.660 --> 00:57:47.340
I had a 50 milliwatt budget.

00:57:47.340 --> 00:57:49.140
I went back and
said, can't do it.

00:57:49.140 --> 00:57:50.010
Can't design it.

00:57:50.010 --> 00:57:51.450
Sorry, I need more power.

00:57:51.450 --> 00:57:54.480
And then they came back and
they said, cool, you get 55.

00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:56.670
Everybody following?

00:57:56.670 --> 00:58:00.000
And then when you
make ui negative,

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:01.890
that's called restricting.

00:58:01.890 --> 00:58:04.110
That's a restriction.

00:58:04.110 --> 00:58:06.660
Because when I said I
wanted five more milliwatts,

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:09.690
presumably there's an overall
budget somewhere of power,

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:12.960
somebody went, whoever my
manager was, went to someone

00:58:12.960 --> 00:58:16.180
designing another part
of the system and said,

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:18.900
hey, can I steal 5
milliwatts from you.

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:20.460
Everybody following this?

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:23.145
And they were like, I guess.

00:58:23.145 --> 00:58:24.480
It's not going to be as good.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:25.290
And we're like,
well, we know it's

00:58:25.290 --> 00:58:27.810
not as good because we're
taking some power away from you.

00:58:27.810 --> 00:58:29.310
But OK, we'll do it.

00:58:29.310 --> 00:58:31.650
And we'll remember
this at bonus time.

00:58:31.650 --> 00:58:33.540
Something like that.

00:58:33.540 --> 00:58:37.370
So that's how this might work.

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:39.730
Equality constraints
it's also super-duper

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:41.170
interesting, a perturbation.

00:58:41.170 --> 00:58:43.750
So here's an example
which is extremely

00:58:43.750 --> 00:58:46.240
practical and universal.

00:58:46.240 --> 00:58:49.150
Let's suppose we're
optimizing an energy system

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.880
so power flows
across, let's say,

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:54.740
the entire California grid.

00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:57.550
Some of the equality
constraints basically

00:58:57.550 --> 00:59:00.250
are flow conservation.

00:59:00.250 --> 00:59:03.970
You point to some
place, some substation,

00:59:03.970 --> 00:59:08.410
and it says basically the
sum of the powers coming in

00:59:08.410 --> 00:59:11.350
from the 115 kilovolt
lines is going

00:59:11.350 --> 00:59:14.440
to equal the sum of the
powers going out over these 15

00:59:14.440 --> 00:59:17.920
kilovolt distribution lines.

00:59:17.920 --> 00:59:20.110
That's power conservation.

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:23.110
Everybody got that?

00:59:23.110 --> 00:59:26.740
So then what happens
if you perturb--

00:59:26.740 --> 00:59:29.320
so 0 here means
power coming in is

00:59:29.320 --> 00:59:31.870
equal to the power going out.

00:59:31.870 --> 00:59:35.630
If you perturb it, if I put
a number there, like plus,

00:59:35.630 --> 00:59:37.130
it actually is weird.

00:59:37.130 --> 00:59:41.450
It basically means, roll a truck
out over to that substation,

00:59:41.450 --> 00:59:45.320
hook up a wire to it, and
either suck some extra power out

00:59:45.320 --> 00:59:46.940
or pump some in.

00:59:46.940 --> 00:59:48.350
Everybody got that?

00:59:48.350 --> 00:59:50.390
Because that's what it means.

00:59:50.390 --> 00:59:54.150
In that case, you're
saying, no the total power--

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:56.840
the net power-- well, let's
say it's the net power going

00:59:56.840 --> 00:59:58.340
into a node and a power system.

00:59:58.340 --> 01:00:01.070
Normally that should be 0.

01:00:01.070 --> 01:00:04.550
But if I say, no, it can be
like a megawatt or something

01:00:04.550 --> 01:00:05.090
like that.

01:00:05.090 --> 01:00:07.640
If it's a megawatt, that
means vi is non-zero.

01:00:07.640 --> 01:00:11.210
And what that means is the
sum of the powers of-- what

01:00:11.210 --> 01:00:14.750
that means is I'm sucking out
1 megawatt from that node.

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:16.520
Everybody got that?

01:00:16.520 --> 01:00:18.240
So actually keep
that idea in mind.

01:00:18.240 --> 01:00:21.170
And this is just to say
what perturbations are.

01:00:21.170 --> 01:00:23.610
And perturbations are
obvious in other areas,

01:00:23.610 --> 01:00:25.490
as well, what they mean.

01:00:25.490 --> 01:00:28.910
But they always mean
something interesting.

01:00:28.910 --> 01:00:33.450
So we are interested in
this perturbed problem.

01:00:33.450 --> 01:00:35.910
And actually, we're super
interested in what happens

01:00:35.910 --> 01:00:37.710
with the optimal value--

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:41.750
what happens to the optimal
value when you have non-zero

01:00:41.750 --> 01:00:43.110
ui's and v's.

01:00:43.110 --> 01:00:44.580
So let's talk about that.

01:00:44.580 --> 01:00:46.860
In this case, if
I increase ui, you

01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:49.560
tell me what happens to the
optimal value of this thing?

01:00:54.050 --> 01:00:58.080
If the ui's are positive, what?

01:00:58.080 --> 01:00:59.160
[INAUDIBLE] down.

01:00:59.160 --> 01:01:00.360
It's got to go down.

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:02.940
Well, OK, it could
stay the same.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:05.970
By the way, it would stay the
same if all of the inequality--

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:09.600
if that inequality
constraint was slack.

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:10.960
You agree with that right?

01:01:10.960 --> 01:01:14.710
You'd say, I had a
50-milliwatt thing.

01:01:14.710 --> 01:01:16.660
And I just did my
power analysis.

01:01:16.660 --> 01:01:18.130
I'm coming in at 48 milliwatts.

01:01:18.130 --> 01:01:19.460
Everybody following this?

01:01:19.460 --> 01:01:22.730
And then somebody comes to
me and says, fantastic news.

01:01:22.730 --> 01:01:24.700
I've got the best
news ever for you.

01:01:24.700 --> 01:01:26.620
Remember I said you had
50 milliwatt budget?

01:01:26.620 --> 01:01:29.370
I'm giving you 55.

01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:33.640
And what is the
consequence in that case?

01:01:33.640 --> 01:01:34.935
There's no consequence at all.

01:01:34.935 --> 01:01:37.775
You're like, I don't need it
because I'm coming in at 48.

01:01:37.775 --> 01:01:39.150
I don't even need
your stupid 50.

01:01:39.150 --> 01:01:41.225
Here, take 2 back.

01:01:41.225 --> 01:01:41.850
Don't say that.

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:43.890
But you could say something
like that if you wanted

01:01:43.890 --> 01:01:45.557
and that would be
completely reasonable.

01:01:45.557 --> 01:01:48.850
Everybody following this?

01:01:48.850 --> 01:01:51.435
And if ui is
negative, and so you

01:01:51.435 --> 01:01:53.810
tighten the constraint, what
happens to the optimal value

01:01:53.810 --> 01:01:54.477
of this problem?

01:01:57.160 --> 01:01:58.570
It's going to go up.

01:01:58.570 --> 01:02:00.670
Once again, if the
constraint is slack,

01:02:00.670 --> 01:02:04.510
it might not go up at all
because they go, yep, sorry,

01:02:04.510 --> 01:02:07.660
I'm dropping you from 50
milliwatt budget to 49.

01:02:07.660 --> 01:02:09.940
And you go, well, OK, whatever.

01:02:09.940 --> 01:02:12.520
They go, aren't you going
to redesign your part?

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:15.040
And you go like, no, because
my current part is only

01:02:15.040 --> 01:02:16.180
coming in at 48.

01:02:16.180 --> 01:02:17.620
Everybody following this?

01:02:17.620 --> 01:02:19.660
But if it were
tight, you're going

01:02:19.660 --> 01:02:22.000
to pay for it, including
quite possibly,

01:02:22.000 --> 01:02:23.770
it'll become infeasible.

01:02:23.770 --> 01:02:25.280
That can happen.

01:02:25.280 --> 01:02:25.780
Yeah?

01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:27.050
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:27.050 --> 01:02:27.550
Yeah.

01:02:27.550 --> 01:02:31.270
So that's a strong form
of an increase in p star.

01:02:31.270 --> 01:02:34.090
This p star will
become infinity.

01:02:34.090 --> 01:02:36.700
So can happen.

01:02:36.700 --> 01:02:40.450
So what happens here
is if you work out

01:02:40.450 --> 01:02:43.750
the dual of this problem, that's
that, and here's the dual here.

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:50.190
And this is only because we've
shifted these things like that.

01:02:50.190 --> 01:02:53.850
So we're interested
in what this--

01:02:53.850 --> 01:02:56.580
oh, and by the way if there's
only just one of these,

01:02:56.580 --> 01:03:00.570
then the way you do this
is you just plot it.

01:03:00.570 --> 01:03:02.790
And that would be called
a trade-off curve.

01:03:02.790 --> 01:03:05.310
Suppose, I don't know,
here, this is power.

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:08.460
Then you simply
resynthesize the circuit

01:03:08.460 --> 01:03:12.840
with power budgets running
with a the limit running

01:03:12.840 --> 01:03:14.610
from 10 milliwatts to 80.

01:03:14.610 --> 01:03:15.690
And you get a curve.

01:03:15.690 --> 01:03:17.148
And someone would
say, what's that?

01:03:17.148 --> 01:03:19.710
And you go, that's the
optimal trade-off curve

01:03:19.710 --> 01:03:23.718
of my objective with respect
to the power I am allocated.

01:03:23.718 --> 01:03:24.510
Everybody got that?

01:03:24.510 --> 01:03:26.830
So you do something like that.

01:03:26.830 --> 01:03:27.750
So there's just one.

01:03:27.750 --> 01:03:29.910
And then that's literally
just this thing here.

01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:32.700
That's when there's
only just one variable.

01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:35.100
And it requires you to solve
a whole bunch of problems.

01:03:35.100 --> 01:03:38.070
So no problem.

01:03:38.070 --> 01:03:40.860
But here we're going to get a
lot of information, actually

01:03:40.860 --> 01:03:43.170
local information for free.

01:03:43.170 --> 01:03:45.080
And it's this.

01:03:45.080 --> 01:03:52.800
So it turns out that weak
duality here just gives you

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.540
a very cool inequality.

01:03:54.540 --> 01:03:57.990
It says that the
perturbed optimal cost

01:03:57.990 --> 01:04:02.310
is bigger than or equal to
the unperturbed optimal cost

01:04:02.310 --> 01:04:04.205
minus these things.

01:04:04.205 --> 01:04:04.830
And this weird.

01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:06.190
This is global.

01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:07.435
This is not local.

01:04:07.435 --> 01:04:08.310
It's not approximate.

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:09.300
It is global.

01:04:09.300 --> 01:04:11.220
And in fact, it
kind of makes sense

01:04:11.220 --> 01:04:15.060
because look at this here.

01:04:15.060 --> 01:04:18.420
This curve, that's p star.

01:04:18.420 --> 01:04:20.190
This is p star of u, this curve.

01:04:20.190 --> 01:04:21.780
This is u.

01:04:21.780 --> 01:04:23.910
And this curve is p star.

01:04:23.910 --> 01:04:26.460
And what this says,
this thing here

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:30.180
is exactly the right
hand side there.

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:32.603
It's essentially this
tangent plane there.

01:04:32.603 --> 01:04:34.020
And what it says--
and you can see

01:04:34.020 --> 01:04:35.270
it's actually kind of correct.

01:04:35.270 --> 01:04:39.210
This says if I
tighten your budget,

01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:42.440
so I'd have ui negative,
you go over here.

01:04:42.440 --> 01:04:45.170
This says it's got to go
up at least this amount.

01:04:45.170 --> 01:04:48.410
And of course, it's convex so it
goes up more than that amount.

01:04:48.410 --> 01:04:49.340
Everybody got that?

01:04:49.340 --> 01:04:53.060
Including, by the way, it
can go up to plus infinity.

01:04:53.060 --> 01:04:58.310
On the other hand, if I give
you more budget, if I say here,

01:04:58.310 --> 01:05:00.980
have some more
resources, you'll see

01:05:00.980 --> 01:05:05.870
this makes a prediction that the
objective is going to go down.

01:05:05.870 --> 01:05:09.350
The amount the objective goes
down can't be more than that

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:11.380
but it could be less.

01:05:11.380 --> 01:05:14.920
Everybody got this?

01:05:14.920 --> 01:05:17.690
So this is super useful.

01:05:17.690 --> 01:05:19.900
And it's a weird and asymmetric.

01:05:19.900 --> 01:05:22.050
So you need to spend some time.

01:05:22.050 --> 01:05:25.150
Basically go to a quiet
place and look at this

01:05:25.150 --> 01:05:27.310
because it's got all
sorts of implications.

01:05:27.310 --> 01:05:28.390
Some are obvious.

01:05:28.390 --> 01:05:32.120
But it's weird and asymmetric
because of this inequality.

01:05:32.120 --> 01:05:36.440
So it basically says
if I have a large--

01:05:36.440 --> 01:05:39.760
if I have a constraint
that has a large--

01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:43.030
inequality constraint with a
large Lagrange multiplier--

01:05:43.030 --> 01:05:44.740
and you have to
listen to the English

01:05:44.740 --> 01:05:48.280
it's because it's going to
be very carefully worded.

01:05:48.280 --> 01:05:53.940
It says basically, in that case,
if you restrict that problem,

01:05:53.940 --> 01:05:58.785
then your optimal objective
will for sure go up.

01:05:58.785 --> 01:06:01.022
And someone will say, did
you want to re-solve it?

01:06:01.022 --> 01:06:02.730
And you go, well,
number one, no, thanks,

01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:05.520
because it takes me 12 hours
to synthesize the circuit.

01:06:05.520 --> 01:06:07.710
But number two,
you don't have to.

01:06:07.710 --> 01:06:10.230
And you'd say it's going to
go up at least by this amount.

01:06:10.230 --> 01:06:12.022
And someone would say,
could it go up more?

01:06:12.022 --> 01:06:14.160
And you go, yeah, sure,
it could go up more.

01:06:14.160 --> 01:06:16.740
But it can't go up less.

01:06:16.740 --> 01:06:18.720
They'd say, could it
go up to plus infinity?

01:06:18.720 --> 01:06:20.820
And the answer
is, yep, it could.

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:21.780
It absolutely could.

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:23.340
Everybody following this?

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:27.030
Now on the other
hand, it's asymmetric.

01:06:27.030 --> 01:06:32.790
If lambda i star is large
and then I say to you,

01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:34.870
I'm going to give you
some more resources.

01:06:34.870 --> 01:06:37.320
First of all, you
should be happy.

01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:38.760
But when you do
that and I ask you

01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:41.320
what's going to happen
to your p star--

01:06:41.320 --> 01:06:42.820
I mean, you could say a couple--

01:06:42.820 --> 01:06:45.153
I mean, let's be very careful
about what we're saying.

01:06:45.153 --> 01:06:47.320
Here would be the correct
and careful way to say it.

01:06:47.320 --> 01:06:49.510
You'd say, first
of all, number one,

01:06:49.510 --> 01:06:51.573
thank you for the
additional resources.

01:06:51.573 --> 01:06:52.490
That's very important.

01:06:52.490 --> 01:06:53.800
You have to say that first.

01:06:53.800 --> 01:06:55.370
So you say that.

01:06:55.370 --> 01:06:56.540
Then here's what happens.

01:06:56.540 --> 01:06:57.350
And this is real.

01:06:57.350 --> 01:07:01.430
Then they'd say,
great, use it well.

01:07:01.430 --> 01:07:02.450
And you go, oh, I will.

01:07:02.450 --> 01:07:05.565
And they'd say, what's going
to happen to your objective?

01:07:05.565 --> 01:07:07.190
And then you would
say, great question.

01:07:07.190 --> 01:07:09.320
Good news, lambda
star is positive.

01:07:09.320 --> 01:07:14.310
You'd say, well I predict it's
going to go down quite a bit.

01:07:14.310 --> 01:07:16.670
And they go, what
do you mean predict?

01:07:16.670 --> 01:07:18.940
I just gave you
some more resources.

01:07:18.940 --> 01:07:21.910
And then you'd say,
OK, I predict it.

01:07:21.910 --> 01:07:23.230
It probably will.

01:07:23.230 --> 01:07:26.590
But I can't guarantee it because
the actual decrease might

01:07:26.590 --> 01:07:29.830
be less than what I predict.

01:07:29.830 --> 01:07:30.910
Everyone got that?

01:07:30.910 --> 01:07:34.570
And in fact, you could easily
imagine, I'll draw a sick--

01:07:34.570 --> 01:07:38.260
and I'll draw a sad case here
that goes like this here.

01:07:38.260 --> 01:07:41.050
Here's a sad case.

01:07:41.050 --> 01:07:44.290
You do this like this.

01:07:44.290 --> 01:07:47.780
And then this thing
looks like this.

01:07:47.780 --> 01:07:50.000
And that's good enough.

01:07:50.000 --> 01:07:53.770
And then it just
flattens out right there.

01:07:53.770 --> 01:07:56.730
So here's your
Lagrange multiplier.

01:07:56.730 --> 01:07:58.720
Here's your optimal
Lagrange multiplier.

01:07:58.720 --> 01:07:59.980
It's pretty hefty.

01:07:59.980 --> 01:08:03.580
And so whoever is allocating
you more resources

01:08:03.580 --> 01:08:06.970
is expecting your
objective to go down.

01:08:06.970 --> 01:08:08.770
If they give you this
much more, they're

01:08:08.770 --> 01:08:10.760
expecting it to go down to this.

01:08:10.760 --> 01:08:12.580
But what you can see
here is it doesn't.

01:08:12.580 --> 01:08:14.800
Because you didn't know it
but you were just barely

01:08:14.800 --> 01:08:18.100
at the limit when getting
additional resources

01:08:18.100 --> 01:08:19.689
was not going to help you.

01:08:19.689 --> 01:08:21.652
Everybody got this?

01:08:21.652 --> 01:08:24.069
Anyway, so these are weird,
and asymmetric, and very cool,

01:08:24.069 --> 01:08:25.194
and all that kind of stuff.

01:08:25.194 --> 01:08:27.729
And also, this is
now unbelievably--

01:08:27.729 --> 01:08:30.470
this is super useful
because as I said before,

01:08:30.470 --> 01:08:33.350
when you solve a problem,
whether you like it or not,

01:08:33.350 --> 01:08:38.439
you're going to get optimal
Lagrange multipliers.

01:08:38.439 --> 01:08:40.960
And this is awesome because
it allows you to do--

01:08:40.960 --> 01:08:43.479
In fact, I'll tell you a story
about this from a long time

01:08:43.479 --> 01:08:43.979
ago.

01:08:43.979 --> 01:08:46.569
But people are doing
circuit design.

01:08:46.569 --> 01:08:48.399
You have 50 constraints.

01:08:48.399 --> 01:08:51.520
You'd say, oh, my phase
margin has to exceed this.

01:08:51.520 --> 01:08:52.753
I need to be able to do this.

01:08:52.753 --> 01:08:54.170
My power should
be less than that.

01:08:54.170 --> 01:08:55.370
My area should be
less than that.

01:08:55.370 --> 01:08:56.203
This should be that.

01:08:56.203 --> 01:08:57.020
Blah, blah, blah.

01:08:57.020 --> 01:08:58.760
My swing should be this.

01:08:58.760 --> 01:08:59.300
What is it?

01:08:59.300 --> 01:09:02.930
My third order distortion
has to be less than that.

01:09:02.930 --> 01:09:04.130
You have all these things.

01:09:04.130 --> 01:09:07.490
And so you design it.

01:09:07.490 --> 01:09:09.410
Out of your 50 constraints,
it's quite typical

01:09:09.410 --> 01:09:12.229
that only 10 or 15
are going to be tight.

01:09:12.229 --> 01:09:13.939
Everybody following this?

01:09:13.939 --> 01:09:17.930
Then the question is,
well, how tight are they?

01:09:17.930 --> 01:09:21.510
And the Boolean idea of
tightness goes like this.

01:09:21.510 --> 01:09:23.935
It says, you were
allocated 50 milliwatts.

01:09:23.935 --> 01:09:24.810
How much did you use?

01:09:24.810 --> 01:09:27.420
50, you're tight.

01:09:27.420 --> 01:09:30.008
I insisted on a phase
margin of 120 degrees.

01:09:30.008 --> 01:09:31.050
What's your phase margin?

01:09:31.050 --> 01:09:32.899
120.

01:09:32.899 --> 01:09:33.920
Something like that.

01:09:33.920 --> 01:09:35.750
So these are tight.

01:09:35.750 --> 01:09:41.630
But then the question is, you'd
want to know which of these--

01:09:41.630 --> 01:09:43.370
let's get a little
bit more sophisticated

01:09:43.370 --> 01:09:47.210
and say which are more
tight than others.

01:09:47.210 --> 01:09:49.020
Everybody following this?

01:09:49.020 --> 01:09:52.540
And somebody want to
tell me how to do it?

01:09:52.540 --> 01:09:54.790
You just look at the
Lagrange multipliers.

01:09:54.790 --> 01:09:56.380
So if I have a 50 milliwatt--

01:09:56.380 --> 01:10:00.700
I mean assuming everything
is on a similar scale.

01:10:00.700 --> 01:10:04.257
But aside from that, if I have
a power thing, it's tight.

01:10:04.257 --> 01:10:06.340
I'm using all my budget
but my Lagrange multiplier

01:10:06.340 --> 01:10:09.520
is really small, and I have
another constraint which

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:12.340
is tight but with a really
big Lagrange multiplier, then

01:10:12.340 --> 01:10:16.750
what it tells me is the first
one you would say it's tight,

01:10:16.750 --> 01:10:18.080
but it's not that tight.

01:10:18.080 --> 01:10:19.330
Everybody see what I'm saying?

01:10:19.330 --> 01:10:24.250
I mean, actually this is
like in a mechanical system.

01:10:24.250 --> 01:10:26.560
You'd say, yeah, it's
up against the wall

01:10:26.560 --> 01:10:29.020
but there's not very
much contact force there.

01:10:29.020 --> 01:10:31.840
Or that cable is taut
but the there's only

01:10:31.840 --> 01:10:33.760
like 5 newtons of tension on it.

01:10:33.760 --> 01:10:35.070
Everybody see what I'm saying?

01:10:35.070 --> 01:10:39.010
Oh, by the way, now
that you know this,

01:10:39.010 --> 01:10:42.700
I can describe the
mechanical thing well.

01:10:42.700 --> 01:10:45.760
If you minimize the total
energy in a configuration

01:10:45.760 --> 01:10:48.700
of some mechanical thing,
and you have constraints.

01:10:48.700 --> 01:10:52.560
The constraint says that,
for example, two points

01:10:52.560 --> 01:10:54.560
can't be more than a meter
apart because there's

01:10:54.560 --> 01:10:55.560
a cable connecting them.

01:10:55.560 --> 01:10:57.920
Or another one says
there's a floor.

01:10:57.920 --> 01:11:02.460
And you can't have x1,
maybe a position go--

01:11:02.460 --> 01:11:04.220
it can't go below the floor.

01:11:04.220 --> 01:11:05.840
Everybody follow this?

01:11:05.840 --> 01:11:08.750
So then the Lagrange multipliers
for those constraints

01:11:08.750 --> 01:11:12.170
are actually precisely forces.

01:11:12.170 --> 01:11:16.250
Because it's the
partial derivative

01:11:16.250 --> 01:11:21.740
of the optimal energy with
respect to-- when you perturb

01:11:21.740 --> 01:11:23.808
a constraint like that,
it's basically saying,

01:11:23.808 --> 01:11:25.850
I can move the floor down
or something like that.

01:11:25.850 --> 01:11:28.760
And that is actually, literally,
the physical units are Newtons.

01:11:28.760 --> 01:11:31.890
So make sense?

01:11:31.890 --> 01:11:33.170
So it's cool.

01:11:33.170 --> 01:11:39.560
So anyway, this is like these
are all super useful stuff.

01:11:39.560 --> 01:11:45.190
I remember we made a tool
for doing this for circuit

01:11:45.190 --> 01:11:46.690
designers and it ended up--

01:11:46.690 --> 01:11:52.660
you 50 constraints and we
did things like we colored--

01:11:52.660 --> 01:11:54.380
one column would be budget.

01:11:54.380 --> 01:11:55.870
The other would say design.

01:11:55.870 --> 01:11:58.930
So you'd see 50/50, means
you're at your budget.

01:11:58.930 --> 01:12:02.350
We color them red if the
Lagrange multiplier was large,

01:12:02.350 --> 01:12:05.460
orange if it was modest, yellow.

01:12:05.460 --> 01:12:07.210
And there's no color
at all if it's slack.

01:12:07.210 --> 01:12:08.860
Everybody got this?

01:12:08.860 --> 01:12:12.240
Anyway, so circuit designers
were like, that is awesome.

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:14.357
This is new.

01:12:14.357 --> 01:12:15.940
Since when have
people known about ti?

01:12:15.940 --> 01:12:17.210
This is amazing.

01:12:17.210 --> 01:12:18.860
And I said, well, here's a hint.

01:12:18.860 --> 01:12:22.270
These are called
Lagrange multipliers.

01:12:22.270 --> 01:12:24.250
And he died in 1840.

01:12:24.250 --> 01:12:26.620
Anyway, so something
like that anyway.

01:12:26.620 --> 01:12:29.365
I'm just saying, there's a lot
of-- it is all quite useful.

01:12:32.990 --> 01:12:34.880
Now we get to the
local sensitivity.

01:12:34.880 --> 01:12:36.967
So I actually jumped ahead.

01:12:36.967 --> 01:12:37.550
But that's OK.

01:12:37.550 --> 01:12:38.480
That's fine.

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:41.730
So assuming p star
is differentiable,

01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:44.790
which by the way, it often
is not, let's just say that.

01:12:44.790 --> 01:12:47.000
But assuming it
were, you get these--

01:12:47.000 --> 01:12:50.906
and these are the things
most people think about.

01:12:50.906 --> 01:12:53.330
You get that the optimal
Lagrange multipliers are

01:12:53.330 --> 01:12:55.730
very cool partial derivatives.

01:12:55.730 --> 01:12:58.896
For example, this one is--

01:12:58.896 --> 01:13:04.330
the lambda i star is the
partial derivative of--

01:13:04.330 --> 01:13:05.830
it's actually the
partial derivative

01:13:05.830 --> 01:13:08.438
of the optimal cost with
respect to tightening

01:13:08.438 --> 01:13:10.480
that constraint with a
minus sign in front of it.

01:13:10.480 --> 01:13:12.063
Because if you tighten
the constraint,

01:13:12.063 --> 01:13:14.230
cost is going to go up and
then you put a minus sign

01:13:14.230 --> 01:13:14.890
and you get something.

01:13:14.890 --> 01:13:15.790
Anyway, that's it.

01:13:15.790 --> 01:13:19.110
So it's very cool.

01:13:19.110 --> 01:13:22.880
So that's the picture.

01:13:22.880 --> 01:13:26.030
Now I'll go back to
the energy example.

01:13:26.030 --> 01:13:29.540
I have a grid.

01:13:29.540 --> 01:13:32.810
Then what I do is I look
at an equality constraint

01:13:32.810 --> 01:13:35.840
and that equality constraint
says at that node,

01:13:35.840 --> 01:13:38.090
the power going in balances
the power going out.

01:13:38.090 --> 01:13:39.410
Everybody got that?

01:13:39.410 --> 01:13:42.510
And by the way, the inequality
constraint is always tight.

01:13:42.510 --> 01:13:45.280
And I get a Lagrange
multiplier there

01:13:45.280 --> 01:13:48.280
when I solve my
optimization problem.

01:13:48.280 --> 01:13:49.762
I get a Lagrange multiplier.

01:13:49.762 --> 01:13:51.970
And then the Lagrange
multiplier is super interesting

01:13:51.970 --> 01:13:56.230
because it tells you the partial
derivative of the total system

01:13:56.230 --> 01:14:01.130
cost with respect to perturbing
that equality constraint.

01:14:01.130 --> 01:14:02.940
But perturbing an
equality constraint,

01:14:02.940 --> 01:14:06.290
which is a balanced constraint,
that says everything coming

01:14:06.290 --> 01:14:08.150
in balancing
everything going out,

01:14:08.150 --> 01:14:12.110
basically says it's doing the
thought experiment, please

01:14:12.110 --> 01:14:16.820
go hook up some wires and
either suck some energy in--

01:14:16.820 --> 01:14:20.835
suck some energy out of the
system there, or pump some in.

01:14:20.835 --> 01:14:23.210
That's what it means to perturb
that equality constraint.

01:14:23.210 --> 01:14:25.850
What this says, what
the Lagrange multiplier

01:14:25.850 --> 01:14:32.540
tells you then is the increase
or decrease in overall system

01:14:32.540 --> 01:14:36.050
cost if you do that.

01:14:36.050 --> 01:14:37.780
And so in energy,
these are called

01:14:37.780 --> 01:14:41.290
locational marginal prices
because the objective

01:14:41.290 --> 01:14:42.740
is in dollars.

01:14:42.740 --> 01:14:45.280
It's the operating cost
for your 3,000 generators

01:14:45.280 --> 01:14:46.690
or whatever,
something like that.

01:14:46.690 --> 01:14:48.970
Everybody got this?

01:14:48.970 --> 01:14:51.527
So you can look that up.

01:14:51.527 --> 01:14:53.110
If you want, you can
go-- if you want,

01:14:53.110 --> 01:14:55.210
you can even find the
locational marginal prices

01:14:55.210 --> 01:14:57.190
in Texas or in California.

01:14:57.190 --> 01:15:00.700
You specify the node and the
hour or the 15-minute re--

01:15:00.700 --> 01:15:01.990
and you'll get the price.

01:15:01.990 --> 01:15:04.690
It'll be $83 per megawatt hour.

01:15:04.690 --> 01:15:06.940
So everybody follow this?

01:15:06.940 --> 01:15:08.058
And it's just from this.

01:15:08.058 --> 01:15:09.350
And it all makes perfect sense.

01:15:09.350 --> 01:15:10.433
It's really cool, I think.

01:15:13.140 --> 01:15:17.220
Also, this is good for
resource allocation.

01:15:17.220 --> 01:15:20.070
So I'm running a data center.

01:15:20.070 --> 01:15:25.200
I've got 350
different job classes.

01:15:25.200 --> 01:15:27.150
And I want to allocate
resources to them,

01:15:27.150 --> 01:15:30.113
like I/O bandwidth, numbers
of cores, whatever it is.

01:15:30.113 --> 01:15:31.030
These kinds of things.

01:15:31.030 --> 01:15:32.700
And I want to
allocate it to them.

01:15:32.700 --> 01:15:34.710
They're all doing
different things.

01:15:34.710 --> 01:15:37.650
Then you get a really
cool thing where

01:15:37.650 --> 01:15:40.890
I would ask you how
much better will you do

01:15:40.890 --> 01:15:45.240
if I give you more cores,
or more I/O bandwidth,

01:15:45.240 --> 01:15:47.170
or something like that.

01:15:47.170 --> 01:15:49.590
And these would these
literally tell you that--

01:15:49.590 --> 01:15:52.260
these would work out to
the prices of the resources

01:15:52.260 --> 01:15:53.640
in the data center.

01:15:53.640 --> 01:15:55.920
And that's why in
a lot of fields,

01:15:55.920 --> 01:15:58.440
people refer to Lagrange
multipliers or dual variables

01:15:58.440 --> 01:16:03.040
as prices or shadow prices.

01:16:03.040 --> 01:16:03.550
Make sense?

01:16:03.550 --> 01:16:05.693
Which I think is really
good words for this.

01:16:05.693 --> 01:16:07.360
I guess people in
mechanical engineering

01:16:07.360 --> 01:16:11.290
don't because I don't
know how to say.

01:16:11.290 --> 01:16:13.032
For example, I don't know.

01:16:13.032 --> 01:16:14.740
It seems to me these
are just two forces.

01:16:14.740 --> 01:16:16.660
These are just contact forces.

01:16:16.660 --> 01:16:19.090
And they're not prices.

01:16:19.090 --> 01:16:20.140
You say, I'd pay more.

01:16:20.140 --> 01:16:23.830
And you're like, dude,
that's a concrete floor.

01:16:23.830 --> 01:16:26.915
So anyway, those
are the main areas

01:16:26.915 --> 01:16:28.540
where people encounter
all these things

01:16:28.540 --> 01:16:29.957
and have names for
them and stuff.

01:16:32.810 --> 01:16:34.220
We're going to do--

01:16:34.220 --> 01:16:37.040
we're going to look
at one more topic.

01:16:37.040 --> 01:16:39.410
Actually, it's
super interesting.

01:16:39.410 --> 01:16:41.900
And I'll show you what it is.

01:16:41.900 --> 01:16:45.140
And first let me start
with what the issue is.

01:16:45.140 --> 01:16:51.760
So remember that we have
problem reformulations.

01:16:51.760 --> 01:16:54.590
You take a problem and then
you have an equivalent problem.

01:16:54.590 --> 01:16:58.347
And in computer science
you call that a reduction.

01:16:58.347 --> 01:16:59.930
We just call it an
equivalent problem.

01:16:59.930 --> 01:17:04.070
And what it means is if you
can solve the other problem,

01:17:04.070 --> 01:17:05.510
then there's a
way to reconstruct

01:17:05.510 --> 01:17:07.850
the solution of the other one.

01:17:07.850 --> 01:17:10.160
And some of these
transformations are obvious.

01:17:10.160 --> 01:17:14.480
Some aren't, by the way,
but most are, simple ones.

01:17:14.480 --> 01:17:17.060
In fact, anyone's
curious what CVXPy does,

01:17:17.060 --> 01:17:19.130
it does absolutely
nothing but this.

01:17:19.130 --> 01:17:21.360
It starts with your problem.

01:17:21.360 --> 01:17:23.967
It creates a new equivalent
problem, and another one,

01:17:23.967 --> 01:17:25.550
and another one,
another one, another.

01:17:25.550 --> 01:17:29.870
And then 42 steps later,
you arrive at a problem

01:17:29.870 --> 01:17:32.880
which a solver can handle.

01:17:32.880 --> 01:17:34.440
Then you throw it to the solver.

01:17:34.440 --> 01:17:36.600
And then you pull it all
back because every one

01:17:36.600 --> 01:17:39.630
of those reduction methods has
a retrieval method associated

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:42.660
with it which tells
you if you solved

01:17:42.660 --> 01:17:44.965
the thing that I
reduced this to,

01:17:44.965 --> 01:17:46.590
how would I solve
the original problem.

01:17:46.590 --> 01:17:47.680
I mean you didn't
need to follow that.

01:17:47.680 --> 01:17:49.680
But if you're just curious,
that's what it does.

01:17:49.680 --> 01:17:53.310
If you know about compilers,
that's a rewriting system.

01:17:53.310 --> 01:17:59.850
That's how one type of
compiler works that way.

01:17:59.850 --> 01:18:03.070
It takes your source
code, generates

01:18:03.070 --> 01:18:05.200
new source code
that it can prove

01:18:05.200 --> 01:18:07.270
is semantically equivalent.

01:18:07.270 --> 01:18:10.210
So for example, it might
reorder some operations

01:18:10.210 --> 01:18:13.510
but it will only
do that if it knows

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:15.430
that it's going to
preserve the semantics

01:18:15.430 --> 01:18:16.885
of your original program.

01:18:16.885 --> 01:18:18.260
And you can do
that, for example,

01:18:18.260 --> 01:18:20.343
if they don't have anything
to do with each other.

01:18:20.343 --> 01:18:23.958
If no one writes on some
memory, that kind of thing.

01:18:23.958 --> 01:18:25.250
Anyway, you just do that stuff.

01:18:25.250 --> 01:18:27.190
So you just do lots
and lots of rewriting

01:18:27.190 --> 01:18:30.760
and then you end up with--
and lot of modern things

01:18:30.760 --> 01:18:33.910
do exactly that kind of stuff.

01:18:33.910 --> 01:18:35.928
So here's what I
was going to say.

01:18:35.928 --> 01:18:37.720
And maybe we'll do the
main thing yesterday

01:18:37.720 --> 01:18:40.510
but I'll just set
the picture up now.

01:18:40.510 --> 01:18:44.770
So you start with a
problem P. And then

01:18:44.770 --> 01:18:48.020
I'm going to change
it to a new one.

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:49.570
This is the equivalent.

01:18:49.570 --> 01:18:51.340
And this could be
something really dumb,

01:18:51.340 --> 01:18:53.725
like eliminating
equality constraints,

01:18:53.725 --> 01:18:54.850
adding equality constraint.

01:18:54.850 --> 01:18:55.615
I don't know.

01:18:55.615 --> 01:18:56.830
Adding slack variables.

01:18:56.830 --> 01:18:58.210
Just something, right?

01:18:58.210 --> 01:19:01.240
And so you can think
of these as equivalent.

01:19:01.240 --> 01:19:04.490
And I can form a Lagrange
dual here and I get this.

01:19:07.060 --> 01:19:10.540
Well, but I can form the
Lagrange dual of this thing

01:19:10.540 --> 01:19:12.190
and that should be--

01:19:12.190 --> 01:19:16.390
I'll call that D tilde star
or something like that.

01:19:16.390 --> 01:19:17.920
Or no, I'll just
call it D tilde.

01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:20.860
Sorry, there we go.

01:19:20.860 --> 01:19:22.270
Like that.

01:19:22.270 --> 01:19:25.360
And so then you'd say,
look, these two problems

01:19:25.360 --> 01:19:28.230
are intimately connected
because they're equivalent.

01:19:28.230 --> 01:19:29.700
These two are
intimately connected

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:31.710
because as the Lagrange
dual of this one.

01:19:31.710 --> 01:19:35.520
This one is intimately connected
to Lagrange dual of that one.

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:40.260
So then I want to ask
what about these two.

01:19:40.260 --> 01:19:41.760
And here's what you--

01:19:41.760 --> 01:19:44.478
I mean, they are
obviously closely related.

01:19:44.478 --> 01:19:46.020
They all have the
same optimal value,

01:19:46.020 --> 01:19:49.230
I mean assuming
there's strong duality.

01:19:49.230 --> 01:19:51.660
Because this has the same
optimal value as that.

01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:53.858
That's what it means to be an--

01:19:53.858 --> 01:19:56.400
this has the same as that of
strong duality and [INAUDIBLE]..

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:58.470
So this is related
to that one certainly

01:19:58.470 --> 01:19:59.820
through this chain of three.

01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:02.400
But what you really
want to do is

01:20:02.400 --> 01:20:05.800
draw this in and
say that there's

01:20:05.800 --> 01:20:07.730
some connection between them.

01:20:07.730 --> 01:20:09.410
So it sounds great.

01:20:09.410 --> 01:20:13.180
And by the way, people trained
in math will have a huge urge.

01:20:13.180 --> 01:20:15.865
If you show this picture
to someone in math,

01:20:15.865 --> 01:20:18.730
their motor cortex
will start twitching

01:20:18.730 --> 01:20:21.520
because they want to write
in and they want to make

01:20:21.520 --> 01:20:22.880
that a commutative diagram.

01:20:22.880 --> 01:20:24.760
So it's true.

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:26.650
Don't deny it.

01:20:26.650 --> 01:20:28.120
So if you see that
and you show--

01:20:28.120 --> 01:20:29.140
if you go to the math
department and sh--

01:20:29.140 --> 01:20:30.790
they're going to go like--
they're going to be-- they're

01:20:30.790 --> 01:20:32.207
going to be looking
for chalk, OK?

01:20:32.207 --> 01:20:33.140
Not this thing.

01:20:33.140 --> 01:20:35.870
But when they get the chalk,
they're going to draw in a line

01:20:35.870 --> 01:20:36.370
there.

01:20:36.370 --> 01:20:38.350
Anyway so, this
is just to set up

01:20:38.350 --> 01:20:40.980
and I think we'll
quit for today.

